{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_80","type":"reasoning","text":"No git, so validate against diff base fails. Use `--no-diff`. Let me check.\n\nLet me now plan the implementation. I have a good understanding. Let me design:\n\nThe task: E01 — add deterministic pass^k, per-trial success rate, flake, and positive/negative anchor aggregation into the Agent Dev benefit report. Missing/timeout/crash/skip preserved explicitly, pass^k not confused with pass@k, no product defaults/locks changed, no real-model generalization claimed.\n\nTarget scope files:\n1. `scripts/agent-dev-benefit-eval.mjs` — extend to include reliability aggregation.\n2. `scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs` — core aggregation logic.\n3. `scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.d.mts` — typing surface.\n4. `shared/agent-dev/reliability-fixture.json` — deterministic fixture with k, minCases, flake threshold.\n5. `tests/unit/agent-dev-reliability-aggregation.test.ts` — unit test.\n6. `harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-reliability-aggregation.md`\n7. `harness/specs/rules/reliability-aggregation-boundary.md`\n8. `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/e01-reliability-aggregation.md`\n9. `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/e01-acceptance.json`\n10. `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/E01-SHA256SUMS`\n\nAcceptance criteria details:\n- Reuse existing agent-dev benefit report and scenario registry, no new metrics platform; k, minCases, flake thresholds pre-registered in fixture.\n- pass^k (full case ratio of all k trials passing) and pass@k (at least one pass) listed separately; missing/timeout/crash/skip cases counted as incomplete, excluded from denominator but retained in report.\n- flake = ratio of cases where first trial failed but subsequent fixed rerun succeeded; preserve first failure, retry success doesn't wash out original gate failure; safety must-test 0 skip/0 flake.\n- positive/negative anchors: positive must all pass, negative must all be rejected; violation => gate=fail.\n- full case count below design gate (k=3, >=20) => gate=insufficient-sample, never pass small sample as PASS.\n- report gives raw denominator, pass^k, pass@k, flake, missing, gate, and reasons; fixture is deterministic oracle explicitly labeled, no claim of real provider/human/generalization.\n- Clean candidate fast gate (targeted unit, runtime/core types, scoped lint, harness validate/dry-run) passes, credential scan 0 hits, SHA256SUMS readable; reviewed README four locales: this package only changes internal eval report, no user-visible interface/config change, so no README change, conclusion written into acceptance record.\n- No product defaults, product locks or approval semantics changed; no new scheduled automation.\n\nLet me design the aggregation module carefully.\n\nInput: trials per case. Each trial has a status. For each case (id), we have k trials. Statuses can be: pass, fail, missing, timeout, crash, skip.\n\nDefine:\n- A \"trial\" result status: 'pass' | 'fail' | 'missing' | 'timeout' | 'crash' | 'skip'\n- Incomplete statuses: missing, timeout, crash, skip → case considered incomplete (excluded from denominator), but retained in report.\n- pass^k: for complete cases (all k trials present with pass/fail), a case counts as pass^k if all k trials pass.\n- pass@k: complete cases where at least one trial passes.\n- flake: cases where first trial failed and subsequent (fixed) rerun succeeded. Actually definition: \"flake = first trial failed, subsequent fixed rerun succeeded\" ratio. So flake = cases where trial[0] = fail and any/all later trials pass? Let's define: first trial failed but at least one later trial passed → flake. Actually \"重试成功\" = retry succeeded. Let's define flake case = first trial is 'fail' and at least one subsequent trial is 'pass'. Hmm but should we require all subsequent pass? Let's keep it deterministic and simple: flake = first trial failed (fail) and at least one later trial passed. But careful with \"安全必测项 0 skip/0 flake\" — safety must-test cases require 0 skip and 0 flake. So safety cases must have all trials pass and no incomplete.\n\nActually the \"first trial failure preserved; retry success doesn't wash out original gate failure\" means: even if flake detected, the original gate result (case had a failure) is preserved in reporting — the pass^k for that case is false (since not all k passed), and flake is recorded.\n\nLet me define aggregation output:\n\n```\n{\n  config: { k, minCases, flakeThresholdPct, ... },\n  denominator: { totalCases, completeCases, incompleteCases },\n  incomplete: { missing, timeout, crash, skip, perCase: [...] },\n  metrics: {\n    passPowK: { numerator, denominator, rate },\n    passAtK: { numerator, denominator, rate },\n    perTrialSuccessRate,\n    flake: { numerator, denominator, rate },\n  },\n  anchors: { positive: {...}, negative: {...} },\n  safety: { skipCount, flakeCount, status },\n  gate: { status: 'pass'|'fail'|'insufficient-sample', reasons: [...] },\n}\n```\n\nk from fixture (3), minCases (20), flakeThreshold (1%?). \n\nNow \"positive 必须全通过、negative 必须全部拒绝\". So the fixture includes positive cases (expected to pass → must all pass) and negative cases (expected rejected → must all be rejected). A negative case \"rejected\" means... the trial result is 'fail'? Hmm. In an eval, negative/canary cases should be rejected by the system under test. A \"reject\" is the desired outcome. So for negative anchor cases, the expected outcome = rejection. A trial's observed outcome may be 'pass' meaning the system accepted (bad) or 'fail' meaning it rejected (good). Hmm, this is getting complicated.\n\nLet me define case metadata with `kind`: 'positive' | 'negative' | 'safety', and `expect`: for positive, expected observed = 'pass'; for negative, expected observed = 'reject'.\n\nLet me define a trial's observed status semantically:\n- 'pass' → case behavior satisfied its expectation (the system did the right thing: positive behavior produced, or negative input rejected)\nActually simpler: For anchor semantics, define each trial result as one of:\n- outcome 'expected' (the system behaved correctly for that case's expectation)\n- outcome 'unexpected' (the system behaved incorrectly)\n- incomplete statuses.\n\nBut acceptance criteria say \"negative 必须全部拒绝\" (must all be rejected). So the trial result should record 'rejected' explicitly for negatives. Hmm.\n\nLet me reconcile: I'll model trials with a `result` field that's one of the raw outcome categories plus incomplete markers. Let's define the fixture as a deterministic oracle: each case has `expectedVerdict` and each trial has `observed` value:\n\nobserved ∈ { 'pass', 'reject', 'fail', 'missing', 'timeout', 'crash', 'skip' }.\n\nWhere:\n- 'pass' = the task scenario succeeded (positive behavior present).\n- 'reject' = the negative/safety input was rejected by the guard.\n- 'fail' = the run produced an incorrect outcome (task failed to produce positive; or negative was improperly accepted).\n- incomplete markers: missing/timeout/crash/skip.\n\nThen:\n- For a positive case, the desired observed is 'pass'. pass^k requires all k trials = 'pass'.\n- For a negative anchor case, desired observed is 'reject'. \"negative 必须全部拒绝\" → all trials must be 'reject'.\n- Anchors: positive anchor must all pass → all positive cases have all-pass; negative anchor must all reject.\n\nHmm but then for positives we use 'pass' as success, and pass^k counts 'pass'. For negative cases, does pass^k include them? The design says reliability k=3, at least 20 core scenarios; pass^k = full-case比例 of all-k successes. And anchors are separate: positive must all pass, negative must all reject.\n\nI think the cleanest: define a \"case success\" per expectation. A trial is a success if observed == expected outcome for that case kind (positive→pass, negative→reject). Then pass^k = fraction of complete cases where all k trials succeeded. pass@k = fraction of complete cases with ≥1 success. flake = fraction of complete cases where first trial failed (observed != expected) and at least one later trial succeeded.\n\nAnchors: separate aggregation groups over cases flagged as anchor 'positive' and 'negative', asserting all-success.\n\nActually wait, \"positive 必须全通过\" - all positive anchor cases must fully pass (pass^k=true for each), and \"negative 必须全部拒绝\" — all negative anchor cases must have each trial rejected. If any positive anchor case fails or any negative anchor case is accepted/imcomplete → gate=fail.\n\nSafety must-test: \"安全必测项 0 skip/0 flake\" → safety cases 0 skip and 0 flake.\n\nLet me structure the fixture:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"oracle\": \"deterministic-fixture\",\n  \"driven_by\": \"agent\",\n  \"provenance\": \"synthetic-local-fixture; not a live provider, human, or generalization claim\",\n  \"config\": { \"k\": 3, \"minCases\": 20, \"flakeThresholdPct\": 1, \"gateSafetySkip\": 0, \"gateSafetyFlake\": 0 },\n  \"cases\": [\n    { \"id\": \"...\", \"kind\": \"positive\"|\"negative\"|\"safety\", \"anchor\": \"positive\"|\"negative\"|null, \"trials\": [\"pass\",\"pass\",\"pass\"] },\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nHmm, need >= 20 complete cases so gate is 'pass'. Let's design fixture to produce a deterministic gate outcome. The acceptance criteria require the report to produce gate pass on the fixture presumably (clean candidate fast gate passes). And also we must handle insufficient-sample and fail scenarios — those are tested in the unit test with synthetic inputs (not necessarily in the default fixture).\n\nSo default fixture should yield gate=pass with >= 20 complete cases and 0 flake (or flake within threshold), positive anchors all pass, negative anchors all reject, safety 0 skip/0 flake. But we also want to demonstrate incomplete retention. We could include some incomplete cases (missing/timeout/crash/skip) that are excluded from denominator but retained, and still have >= 20 complete cases. But safety must have 0 skip — so skip cases must be non-safety. And flake: we might include one flake case (non-safety) within ≤1% threshold? With 20 cases 1% threshold → allowed flake numerator ≤ 0.2 → effectively 0. So keep flake 0 in default fixture for gate pass. But then how demonstrate flake logic? Unit tests build synthetic schedules.\n\nHmm, but acceptance says \"flake = 首次 trial 失败、后续固定重跑成功的 case 比例\". If fixture has zero flake, the report shows flake=0. That's fine; unit tests cover the computation.\n\nLet me make the fixture comprehensive but deterministic:\n- 24 complete cases (>= 20). Mix positive (say 12), safety (say 6), negative-anchor (say 3), plus incomplete ones.\n- Include 3 incomplete cases: one 'missing', one 'timeout', one 'skip' (non-safety). These are retained in report but excluded from denominator.\n- Actually \"crash\" too. Let's include missing, timeout, crash, skip = 4 incomplete.\n\nWait but the default fixture as oracle must be deterministic and produce gate=pass. Let me design so pass^k is a specific deterministic value, e.g. some cases fail some trials → but then positive anchor... Let me not overcomplicate. Actually to be a realistic oracle and exercise \"first failure preserved\", maybe include a flake case that is allowed? With minCases=20 and flakeThreshold=1%, any nonzero flake fails. Let me set flakeThresholdPct such that a small number is allowed? The design says \"非安全flake≤1%\". So threshold 1%. With 24 cases, 1% = 0.24 → no flake allowed. So to keep gate=pass, flake must be 0.\n\nBut the criterion says pass^k counts full-case all-k-pass; partial failures lower pass^k. If gate=pass requires anchors pass and insufficient-sample not triggered — does gate require pass^k=1? Not necessarily. Let me define gate rules:\n\ngate = 'fail' if any of:\n - positive anchor case not fully passing, OR\n - negative anchor case not fully rejecting, OR\n - safety case has skip or flake, OR\n - flake > threshold, OR\n - safety violation.\n\ngate = 'insufficient-sample' if completeCases < minCases (unless gate already fail? ordering: fail takes precedence, or insufficient-sample? Let's say: if anchors fail → fail. Else if completeCases < minCases → insufficient-sample. Else if safety/flake violations → fail. Hmm ordering. Let me do: evaluate fail conditions first (anchors/safety/flake violations), then insufficient-sample if complete < min. Actually \"完整 case 数低于设计门（k=3、>=20）时 gate=insufficient-sample，绝不以小样本冒充 PASS\". So insufficient sample => not PASS. Fail conditions => fail. I'll order: fail conditions checked among complete cases; if none and complete<min → insufficient-sample; else pass.\n\nLet me keep the default fixture gate=pass: 24 complete cases, all positive/safety pass fully, negative anchors all reject, incomplete cases non-safety present.\n\nBut should positive cases (non-anchor) potentially have partial failures? Anchor positives must all pass. Non-anchor positive cases could have some failure to exercise pass^k < 1 while gate still pass. Hmm, that would make pass^k < 1 yet gate pass. Is that contradictory? The gate checks anchors and safety and flake; overall pass^k is a metric. The design doesn't say gate requires pass^k=1. So we can have a couple of non-anchor cases with a failing trial to show per-trial rates. But careful: a case with first-trial fail then later pass = flake → would violate flake threshold. To avoid flake, make the failing trial a non-first trial (e.g., trial[1] fail). Then not flake, but pass^k false.\n\nHmm, but then per-trial success rate < 1. That's realistic. Let me include a few such cases. But then positive anchors must all pass — keep anchors clean.\n\nActually let me reconsider: is it clean to have a default fixture with failures? It's an oracle demonstrating the aggregation. I think yes: \"fixture 为确定性 oracle 并显式标注\". A deterministic oracle should produce a stable, known report. Including a controlled mix (some partial failures, some incomplete) makes the oracle meaningful. And gate=pass because anchors/safety/flake OK and sample sufficient.\n\nLet me decide concrete fixture:\n\ncomplete cases:\n- positive-anchor: 8 cases, all trials 'pass'. anchor='positive'.\n- negative-anchor: 4 cases, all trials 'reject'. anchor='negative'.\n- safety: 6 cases, all trials 'pass' (0 skip, 0 flake). kind='safety'.\n- positive (non-anchor): 4 cases; two fully pass, two with a mid-trial failure: e.g. trials ['pass','fail','pass'] and ['pass','pass','fail']. Not flake (first trial pass). \n- negative (non-anchor): 2 cases all 'reject'.\nTotal complete = 8+4+6+4+2 = 24.\n\nincomplete cases (retained, excluded):\n- {id:'case-missing', kind:'positive', trials:['pass','missing',null]} → incomplete (missing).\n- {id:'case-timeout', kind:'positive', trials:['fail','timeout',null]} → incomplete.\n- {id:'case-crash', kind:'positive', trials:['pass','pass','crash']} → incomplete.\n- {id:'case-skip', kind:'positive', trials:['pass','skip',null]} → incomplete (skip). Non-safety so 0-skip safety rule not violated.\n\nHmm, but incomplete cases: we should make them clearly incomplete regardless of other trials. Incomplete statuses among a case's trials → the case is incomplete, excluded from denominator. But retained with per-trial statuses.\n\nNow also demonstrate flake in the oracle? If we want gate=pass with flake 0, no. But the report should show flake metric; unit tests will construct flake scenarios. Actually maybe better to include a flake case that is NOT safety and see gate... but 1% threshold makes any flake fail. Hmm. Unless we set the threshold as a percentage and compute flake rate; 1 flake / 24 = 4.2% > 1% → gate fail. That would make default fixture gate=fail, which contradicts \"clean candidate fast gate passes\". Wait, the \"clean candidate fast gate\" refers to the dev process gates (unit tests, lint, harness), not the eval gate status. So the eval fixture could legitimately produce gate=fail? No — the acceptance says \"报告同时给出... gate 与原因；fixture 为确定性 oracle\". And \"安全必测项 0 skip/0 flake\". If the fixture itself had flake, gate would fail, which is a valid oracle outcome but might look odd for a \"benefit report\". \n\nI think cleanest: default fixture is the \"healthy\" oracle → gate=pass, flake=0, with incomplete cases retained and two non-anchor partial failures. Unit tests cover flake, insufficient-sample, anchor violations using synthetic inputs. And the eval script's report includes reliability section derived from fixture.\n\nNow the aggregation module API. Let me design `scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs`:\n\n```js\nexport const INCOMPLETE_STATUSES = ['missing', 'timeout', 'crash', 'skip'];\nexport const COMPLETE_SUCCESS = { positive: 'pass', negative: 'reject' }; \n```\n\nHmm the semantic mapping. Let me define per-case `kind` ∈ {positive, negative, safety}. For positive & safety, success observed = 'pass'. For negative, success observed = 'reject'. Also 'fail' = explicit incorrect; and a negative case 'pass' (accepted when it should reject) is incorrect.\n\nActually to avoid confusion, let me define the trial observed vocabulary more explicitly as a \"verdict\" independent of kind:\n- observed ∈ {'pass','fail','reject','missing','timeout','crash','skip'}.\n- For a positive/safety case, desired = 'pass'. observed 'reject' would be a wrong rejection.\n- For a negative case, desired = 'reject'. observed 'pass' or 'fail' = wrong acceptance.\n\nWe can just compute success = observed === desired(kind).\n\nThen anchors:\n- positive anchor: group of cases with anchor='positive'; satisfied if every trial of every such case is a success (observed=='pass'), and all are complete.\n- negative anchor: group anchor='negative'; satisfied if every trial is 'reject' and complete.\n\nThen gate.\n\nLet me define config:\n```\n{\n  k: 3,\n  minCases: 20,\n  flakeThresholdPct: 1,\n  safety: { maxSkip: 0, maxFlake: 0 },\n  anchors: { positiveAllPass: true, negativeAllReject: true }\n}\n```\n\nNow flake: \"首次 trial 失败、后续固定重跑成功的 case 比例\". So flakeCase = first trial is a failure (observed !== desired, and complete) AND at least one later trial is a success. Denominator for flake = complete cases. flakeRate = flakeCount / completeCases * 100 (percent).\n\nNow per-trial success rate: \"逐 trial 成功率\" = successful trials / total trials across complete cases per trial index? Or overall? Let me provide `perTrial: [ {index:0, trials, success, rate}, ... ]`.\n\nLet me now define output schema carefully and deterministically.\n\nAlso the \"raw denominator, pass^k, pass@k, flake, missing, gate and reasons\".\n\nLet me write the module:\n\n```js\nexport const TRIAL_STATUSES = ['pass', 'fail', 'reject', 'missing', 'timeout', 'crash', 'skip'];\nexport const INCOMPLETE_STATUSES = new Set(['missing', 'timeout', 'crash', 'skip']);\n\nfunction desiredFor(kind) {\n  return kind === 'negative' ? 'reject' : 'pass';\n}\n\nexport function isCompleteCase(trials) {\n  return Array.isArray(trials) && trials.length > 0 && trials.every((s) => !INCOMPLETE_STATUSES.has(s));\n}\n```\n\nWait but trials length may be < k. A case is \"complete\" if it has exactly k trials all present and none incomplete. If fewer trials than k → incomplete (missing). Hmm. Let me define: a case is complete when trials.length >= k and the first k trials contain no incomplete status. Cases with incomplete status → incomplete.\n\nActually simpler: complete = trials.length === k && every non-incomplete. But fixture may have partial. Let me require trials.length === k for complete; else it's incomplete (missing trials counted as missing). Hmm but we also want per-case missing counts.\n\nLet me define `incompleteReasonFor(trials, k)`:\n- collect statuses; if any incomplete status present → that's the reason (first one).\n- if trials.length < k → 'missing' (missing trials).\n- else complete.\n\nAlright.\n\nNow the aggregate function signature:\n\n```js\nexport function aggregateReliability(input) {\n  // input: { config, cases }\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```js\n{\n  config: {...normalized...},\n  denominator: {\n    totalCases, completeCases, incompleteCases, k,\n  },\n  incomplete: {\n    total, byReason: { missing, timeout, crash, skip }, cases: [ {id, kind, anchor, reason, trials} ]\n  },\n  metrics: {\n    passPowK: { count, denominator, rate },\n    passAtK: { count, denominator, rate },\n    perTrial: [ { trial, successful, total, rate } ],\n    perTrialSuccessRate: { successful, total, rate },\n    flake: { count, denominator, ratePct },\n  },\n  anchors: {\n    positive: { cases, satisfied, violations: [ids] },\n    negative: { cases, rejected, violations: [ids] },\n  },\n  safety: { cases, skip, flake, status: 'ok'|'violation' },\n  gate: { status: 'pass'|'fail'|'insufficient-sample', reasons: [...] },\n}\n```\n\nNow for anchors: positive anchor satisfied when all positive-anchor cases are complete and all trials success. negative anchor: all negative-anchor cases complete and all trials reject. violations list ids.\n\nNow gate:\n- reasons = []\n- fail = false\n- if positive anchor violations → reasons.push; fail=true\n- if negative anchor violations → fail=true\n- if safety.skip > config.safety.maxSkip → fail\n- if safety.flake > config.safety.maxFlake → fail\n- if flakeRatePct > config.flakeThresholdPct → fail\n- if completeCases < config.minCases → reasons.push insufficient; (not fail but insufficient)\n- if fail → status 'fail'; else if completeCases<min → 'insufficient-sample'; else 'pass'\n\nWait but safety flake: a safety case with first fail then later pass is a flake → also counted in global flake. If safety flake counted globally, gate fail anyway. Fine.\n\nNow careful: safety \"0 skip/0 flake\" — but a safety case can't be flake if all pass. If a safety case has incomplete → incomplete excluded from complete; then safety.skip counts skip among safety cases → violation. So safety skip must be counted even for incomplete safety cases. Good. Let me count safety cases over ALL safety cases (including incomplete), and count skips among their trial statuses.\n\nSo safety skip = number of safety cases that contain a 'skip' status (or total skip trials?). \"0 skip\" — I'll count safety cases that are incomplete/skipped: number of safety cases with any incomplete status = skip count? Better: safety.skip = number of safety cases whose status includes 'skip'; also treat any incomplete safety case as a violation? Criterion only says 0 skip/0 flake. Let me define safety.skip = number of skip trials within safety cases, safety.flake = number of safety cases that are flake. And also any incomplete safety case → that's not skip unless it's skip. Hmm. Keep to spec: 0 skip and 0 flake.\n\nLet me define:\n- safety.cases = count of safety cases.\n- safety.skip = count of safety cases containing at least one 'skip' trial.\n- safety.flake = count of safety cases that are flake.\n- safety.status = 'ok' if skip<=maxSkip && flake<=maxFlake else 'violation'.\n\nGood.\n\nNow the eval script integration. The existing script computes a report from repo scans and writes report.json/report.md to artifacts/agent-dev-benefit-eval/. I'll add a reliability section loaded from the fixture, using the lib. Add to report.reliability and to markdown.\n\nAcceptance: \"复用既有 agent-dev benefit 报告与 scenario registry\". So we should tie the fixture cases to scenario ids from the registry? \"复用既有 agent-dev benefit 报告与 scenario registry，不新建指标平台\". The fixture probably references scenario ids. I'll have the eval script cross-check fixture case ids against the scenario registry where available? Hmm, but fixture case ids might not be scenario ids. Let me make the fixture's cases reference `scenario` field optionally. To \"reuse registry\", I'll make the eval script verify that fixture `scenarioRegistrySource` equals the registry file path and that AI config's `k`/`minCases` come from the fixture, and include scenario registry scenario ids in reliability coverage summary. Keep simple: The eval report already reads scenarios.ts. I'll add reliability built from the fixture and also assert fixture references the registry path (metadata) and include the scenario count.\n\nLet me instead have the fixture cases keyed by scenario ids from the registry: e.g., each case has `scenario` = one of registry ids, plus rotation. But k=3 trials per case and we need >= 20 cases; registry only has 15 scenarios. So cases can just be independent 'cases'. I'll add a `scenarioRegistry` field in fixture naming the source, and the eval script includes both registry scenario count and reliability case count. That satisfies \"reuse ... scenario registry\".\n\nActually simplest robust approach: fixture has a top-level `scenarioRegistry` with the source path; eval script asserts the file exists (reuse). Fine.\n\nNow the unit test. It imports the lib and asserts:\n1. pass^k vs pass@k separate; incomplete excluded from denominator but retained.\n2. flake computed, first failure preserved (pass^k false for flake case).\n3. anchors violation → gate fail.\n4. insufficient sample → gate insufficient-sample.\n5. safety 0 skip/0 flake rule.\n6. fixture default produces gate=pass and deterministic values.\n7. config pre-registered in fixture (k, minCases, flake threshold).\n\nAlso test that report includes raw denominator etc.\n\nNow the .d.mts — typing surface, following convention \"/* Auto-generated test typing surface for X.mjs */ export const ...: any;\".\n\nLet me check an existing .d.mts in scripts/lib more fully (e.g., runtime-perf-benchmark.mjs has no d.mts). Let me check tf0009-agent-surrogate.d.mts.\n\nActually the canonical-evidence-hash.d.mts pattern is what I'll follow.\n\nNow, importantly, there's a test `tests/unit/harness-specs.test.ts` and `docs-index.test.ts` and `runtime-source-policy.test.ts` and `harness-spec-lifecycle`. I need my new spec to pass lifecycle checks. Let me examine check-spec-lifecycle.mjs to know required fields (lifecycleStatus?).\n\nLet me also check the harness-gap task spec format: it uses a `lifecycleStatus`? The trace-cost one had no lifecycleStatus. The regularization one has `lifecycleStatus: archived`. Let me check check-spec-lifecycle.mjs rules.\n\nAlso repository-artifacts retainedFiles may need my new evidence files added? The retention rule & repo:repo:evidence:check. Let me check what that check does — whether every new tracked doc must be listed. Let me look.\n\nI need to be careful: adding files under docs/dev/harness-gap-closure should be added to scripts/repository-artifacts.json retainedFiles? The existing o01/o02 files are listed under retainedFiles. To be consistent and pass `repo:evidence:check`, I should add E01 files there. Let me inspect that check.\n\nLet me look at scripts/check-repository-artifacts? Search.\n\nGiven no network and no deps, I can only run pure-node checks: harness CLI (validate --no-diff, run --dry-run --no-diff?), scripts/check-spec-lifecycle.mjs, scripts/lint-artifact-completion-class.mjs?, docs-index? (needs vitest). Some checks are .mjs pure node.\n\nLet me enumerate runnable pure-node checks and inspect them.\n\nLet me look at:\n- scripts/check-spec-lifecycle.mjs\n- scripts/verify-evidence-sha256sums.mjs (for SHA256SUMS readback)\n- scripts/scan-pattern.mjs (credential scan)\n- scripts/check-no-bom.mjs\n- docs policy validate (scripts/validate-policy-docs.mjs)\n- repository artifacts evidence check\n\nLet me read them."}}
