harness/specs/rules/reliability-aggregation-boundary.md` — must have id/title/type. Rule specs are loaded by loadRuleSpecs (harness/specs/rules/*.md). If it lacks proper frontmatter, `harness list` would throw? It parses frontmatter for every rule. So give it `id`, `title`, `type: ai-coding-rule`, `severity`. Like trace-cost-sampling-boundary.\n\nAlso `harness context --spec <taskspec>` requires scenarios/rules referenced to exist. My task spec's requiredRules should reference existing rule ids: `reliability-aggregation-boundary`, `docs-sync`, `task-forest-autoclose-and-roles`, `repository-artifact-retention`, maybe `agent-dev-conformance-evidence`. And scenario gateway-backend-communication. context will attempt to load them; if missing → missingRules. My new rule will exist. Good.\n\nNow also: new rule spec will be picked up by `harness list`. And `harness:ci` runs `repo:duplicates:check` etc. Not runnable without deps.\n\nAlso there's `pnpm harness validate --spec harness/specs/scenarios/gateway-backend-communication.md` in harness:ci — the scenario spec is fixed; adding a task doesn't affect it.\n\nNow, will adding a task spec with scenario gateway cause `harness:ci` (which validates the gateway scenario example spec and the fix-chat-history example) to break? No.\n\nBut: does any existing check enumerate ALL task specs and require them to validate? `check-spec-lifecycle` does (lifecycleStatus). `repo:artifacts:check` checks docs.files tracked. `harness:specs:lifecycle` = check-spec-lifecycle. There's also possibly a test `harness-specs.test.ts` or `harness-spec-lifecycle.test.ts`. Let me check harness-spec-lifecycle.test.ts.\n\nLet me check tests/unit/harness-spec-lifecycle.test.ts and docs-index (top-level docs only, my new docs are nested → fine).\n\nLet me look."}}
