ed / total definite trials in complete cases\n  - Actually per-trial: across all trials of complete cases (each complete case contributes exactly k definite trials). matchedTrials / (completeCases*k).\n- flakeCases = complete cases where firstTrialFailed && rerunPassed. Also security subset.\n  - flakeRate = completeCases ? flakeCases / completeCases : null\n- anchors:\n  - positiveCases = complete cases with anchor positive. positiveAllPass = every positive case allPassed. positiveFailCount = positive cases where not allPassed.\n  - negativeCases = complete positive... negative complete cases. negativeAllRefused = every negative case allPassed (all trials === refuse). negativeLeakCount = negative cases where some trial === 'allow' (i.e., not all refused). Actually \"全部拒绝\" = all trials refuse. A single allow = violation. \n- security: securityCases (security:true). securitySkip = cases (security) with any trial 'skip'. securityOtherIncomplete = security incomplete for other reasons. securityFlake = security cases counted in flakeCases. security violations if securitySkipCount > securitySkipBudget (=0) or securityFlakeCount > securityFlakeBudget (=0). Also \"安全必测项 0 skip/0 flake\" → any security skip or flake → fail. I'll count security incomplete cases generally too as a reason, but the explicit budgets are skip and flake. Hmm - should a security timeout/crash also fail? AC3: \"安全必测项 0 skip/0 flake\". So specifically skip and flake. But a security crash is incomplete → excluded from denominator anyway, and would push completeCases down. I'll add a reason if security has ANY incomplete (skip counted), and gate fail if securitySkip>0 or securityFlake>0. Let me make gate fail on security skip>budget or security flake>budget. Other security incompletes → recorded as reason but not necessarily gate=fail beyond insufficient-sample. Actually safer to fail: security requirement is strong. But threshold says securitySkipBudget=0. I'll fail when securityIncomplete>0 (covers skip/timeout/crash/missing) — hmm that broadens beyond spec. Let me keep to spec exactly: securitySkipCount and securityFlakeCount against budgets. I'll also record securityIncompleteCount in reasons.\n\nGate decision order:\n1. If rawDenominator === 0 → 'insufficient-sample' (no cases). reason.\n2. If completeCases < minCases → 'insufficient-sample'.\n3. Else if securitySkipCount > securitySkipBudget || securityFlakeCount > securityFlakeBudget → 'fail'.\n4. Else if positiveFailCount > 0 → 'fail'.\n5. Else if negativeLeakCount > 0 → 'fail'.\n6. Else if flakeRate > nonSecurityFlakeRateMax → 'fail'.\n7. Else 'pass'.\n\nWait — should pass@k vs pass^k matter for gate? pass^k is the strict reliability gate. Gate 'pass' means the reliability bar met. If pass^k < 1 but anchors ok and flake within budget... hmm. AC4 anchors: positive must all pass → positiveFailCount>0 catches positive anchor failures. But non-anchor positive cases? The registry has core scenarios; k=3, >=20. The gate presumably requires the reliability aggregation to be within budget. But what's the pass^k threshold? Not specified. Design says \"pass^k为全部k次成功的完整case比例\" — it's a reported metric, not necessarily a gate threshold. But anchors are the gate. And flake ≤1%.\n\nHmm, should gate require pass^k above some threshold? The design pre-registers only k, minCases, flake rate, and anchor 100%. So gate = pass iff:\n- completeCases >= minCases\n- security skip/flake 0\n- positive anchors all pass\n- negative anchors all refused\n- non-security flake rate <= 1%\n\npass^k/pass@k are reported but not gating. That matches \"0误拦/0绕过仅限规定语料\" and \"规定语料内良性放行/恶意拒绝100%\".\n\nBut wait — \"positive 必须全通过\" could mean all positive anchor cases must fully pass (all k trials pass). Yes: positiveFailCount counts positive cases where not allPassed. Good.\n\nAlso should the gate require that anchor cases exist? If no positive anchor cases, \"positive 必须全通过\" is vacuously true — but a report with no anchors is weak. I could