{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_144","type":"reasoning","text":"The benefit-eval script works standalone. My plan is:\n\n1. Create `scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs` — pure-Node aggregation library exporting functions to compute pass^k, pass@k, per-trial success rates, flake, incomplete handling, positive/negative anchors, and a gate decision.\n2. Create `scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.d.mts` — auto-generated-style typing surface.\n3. Create `shared/agent-dev/reliability-fixture.json` — deterministic oracle fixture with pre-registered k=3, minCases=20, flake thresholds, and a set of cases/trials.\n4. Integrate the aggregation into `scripts/agent-dev-benefit-eval.mjs` (§objective: \"在 Agent Dev benefit 报告里加入确定性的 pass^k...\") — add an `reliability` section computed from the fixture and registry; keep existing report intact.\n5. Create `tests/unit/agent-dev-reliability-aggregation.test.ts` — unit tests for the aggregation module and the report integration.\n6. Create `harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-reliability-aggregation.md` — task spec with valid frontmatter (lifecycleStatus, scenario, taskType, etc.).\n7. Create `harness/specs/rules/reliability-aggregation-boundary.md` — rule spec.\n8. Create `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/e01-reliability-aggregation.md` — report.\n9. Create `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/e01-acceptance.json` — structured acceptance record.\n10. Create `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/E01-SHA256SUMS` — checksums of retained files.\n11. Update `.gitignore` and `scripts/repository-artifacts.json` to retain the new durable files.\n\nWait — the target scope is given. Let me re-read it:\n\n```\n\"targetScope\": [\n  \"scripts/agent-dev-benefit-eval.mjs\",\n  \"scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs\",\n  \"scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.d.mts\",\n  \"shared/agent-dev/reliability-fixture.json\",\n  \"tests/unit/agent-dev-reliability-aggregation.test.ts\",\n  \"harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-reliability-aggregation.md\",\n  \"harness/specs/rules/reliability-aggregation-boundary.md\",\n  \"docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/e01-reliability-aggregation.md\",\n  \"docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/e01-acceptance.json\",\n  \"docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/E01-SHA256SUMS\"\n]\n```\n\nThe targetScope doesn't include `.gitignore` or `scripts/repository-artifacts.json`. Hmm, but the acceptance criteria mention \"SHA256SUMS 可回读\" and \"凭据扫描 0 命中\". And the task spec needs touchedAreas/ownedPaths to cover changed files (harness validate). But since the harness needs a diff base and there's no git, `validate --no-diff` would skip ownership. Actually ownership check is skipped with `--no-diff` only for changedFiles (empty). So no failure.\n\nBut wait: `validateSpecPortability` runs even in no-diff. And spec-lifecycle requires lifecycleStatus on every task spec.\n\nAlso the task spec's frontmatter for a gateway-backend-communication task REQUIRES scenario: gateway-backend-communication, taskType: runtime-bridge, requiredProfiles including fast+comms, and requiredRules including renderer-main-boundary, backend-communication-boundary, comms-regression, docs-sync. Hmm, that's if isGatewayBackendCommunicationTask. If I set scenario to gateway-backend-communication then validate demands those. But this task doesn't touch communication paths — it's a local evaluation report. \n\nLet me look at the harness-gap-closure-plan spec: it has no scenario/taskType, and it validated fine (it's not a gateway task). Its requiredRules were task-forest-autoclose-and-roles, docs-sync. And requiredProfiles: [fast]. So a scenario-less task spec is acceptable.\n\nBut the source-inventory E01 packet says spec path `harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-reliability.md` — but the target scope explicitly says `harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-reliability-aggregation.md`. I'll go with the target scope path.\n\nGiven the AGENTS.md \"Spec-driven harness rule\": AI Coding tasks touching backend communication must start from task spec referencing gateway-backend-communication. This task doesn't touch communication; it's a local benefit-report evaluation. So a scenario-less spec like harness-gap-closure-plan is the right model.\n\nHowever, the acceptance criteria item 7 says: \"已复核 README 四语：本包仅改内部评测报告，无用户可见接口/配置变化，故不改 README\". So docs.required can be true with the closure doc, and a note about README.\n\nLet me design the reliability aggregation module carefully.\n\n### Fixture design\n\n`shared/agent-dev/reliability-fixture.json`:\nDefine the pre-registered thresholds and deterministic oracle cases.\n\nStructure:\n```json\n{\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n  \"id\": \"agent-dev-reliability-fixture-v1\",\n  \"driven_by\": \"agent\",\n  \"oracle\": \"deterministic-fixture\",\n  \"note\": \"Synthetic deterministic oracle; not a real provider, human, or generalization claim.\",\n  \"preRegistered\": {\n    \"k\": 3,\n    \"minCases\": 20,\n    \"nonSecurityFlakeRateMax\": 0.01,\n    \"securitySkipBudget\": 0,\n    \"securityFlakeBudget\": 0,\n    \"source\": \"docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/source-inventory.json#experimentParameters.reliability\"\n  },\n  \"cases\": [\n    { \"id\": \"...\", \"kind\": \"positive\" | \"negative\" | \"security\", \"trials\": [\"pass\",\"pass\",\"pass\"] | ... }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nTrial outcomes: \"pass\", \"fail\", \"timeout\", \"crash\", \"skip\", \"missing\"? \"missing\" == no trial recorded. Let me define outcome vocabulary: `pass`, `fail`, `timeout`, `crash`, `skip`, `missing`.\n\nCase classification:\n- complete case = all k trials are recorded terminal pass/fail (i.e., no incomplete outcome). Only `pass`/`fail` are \"complete\" trial results; `timeout`/`crash`/`skip`/`missing` make the case incomplete.\n- pass^k case = complete AND all k trials pass.\n- pass@k case = complete AND at least one trial passes? Actually pass@k typically is fraction of cases where at least one of k passed. The spec says \"pass@k（至少一次通过）\". Keep to complete cases for the denominator to keep it comparable? The criteria says missing/timeout/crash/skip cases counted as incomplete, excluded from denominator, preserved in the report. So both pass^k and pass@k denominators = complete cases.\n- flake = case where first trial failed but subsequent fixed rerun succeeds. Definition: \"flake = 首次 trial 失败、后续固定重跑成功的 case 比例；保留首次失败，重试成功不洗掉原门失败\". So flake = cases where trial[0] is fail and at least one later trial passes. Denominator = complete cases (or cases that had a first failure?). Let me define: flake cases = complete cases whose first trial is `fail` and some later trial is `pass`. Rate = flake-cases / complete-cases. Report both count and rate.\n\nActually more precisely: flake ratio = (# cases where first trial failed and subsequent rerun succeeded) / (# complete cases). Let's keep denominator = complete cases.\n\n- positive anchor: positive cases must all pass ⇒ all k trials pass for every positive case. If any positive case fails → gate=fail.\n- negative anchor: negative cases must all be rejected. \"negative 必须全部拒绝\" — i.e., for negative/security cases, the expected outcome is \"rejected\". Hmm. In an eval, a negative anchor case passes when the system rejects it. So how do we encode? Let's define case.kind: `positive` (expected pass), `negative` (expected reject), `security` (must-test, expected reject, 0 skip/flake budget).\n\nFor negative cases: \"全部拒绝\" means every negative trial must be a rejection. Represent expected = 'reject'. A negative case passes when the trial outcome is `reject`. Hmm, but I've defined outcomes pass/fail. Let me add `reject` as an outcome meaning the system correctly refused. Actually let me reconsider: for an anchor, \"positive\" case => expected allow/pass. \"negative\" => expected reject.\n\nLet me define trial outcome as the *observed* behavior:\n- `pass` — expected behavior observed (for positive: task succeeded; for negative: rejected).\n- `fail` — behavior violated expectation.\nPlus incomplete: timeout/crash/skip/missing.\n\nThat gets messy because pass means different things. Cleaner: encode each case with an `expect` field (`allow`/`reject`/`any`) and each trial with an `outcome` field among `allow`/`reject`/`timeout`/`crash`/`skip`/`missing`. Then:\n- trial \"pass\" = outcome matches case.expect.\n- For positive cases, expect=allow.\n- For negative/security cases, expect=reject.\n\nThen pass^k = complete cases where all k trials are correct (pass). pass@k = complete cases where >= 1 trial correct. This unifies positive and negative. And \"positive 必须全通过\" = every positive case all-pass; \"negative 必须全部拒绝\" = every negative case all trials reject (outcome reject == expect reject == pass).\n\nHmm, \"全部拒绝\" = all reject. So for negative case, all k trials must be `reject`. If a negative trial is `allow`, that's a violation → gate fail.\n\nThis is clean. Let me define:\n\nFixture case:\n```\n{\n  \"id\": \"pos-login-001\",\n  \"kind\": \"positive\" | \"negative\" | \"security\",\n  \"expect\": \"allow\" | \"reject\",\n  \"trials\": [{\"trial\": 1, \"outcome\": \"allow\"}, ...]  // or simpler: outcome strings array\n}\n```\n\nSimplify: `\"trials\": [\"allow\",\"allow\",\"allow\"]`. And `expect` derived from kind? Positive → allow; negative/security → reject. I'll keep explicit `expect` too for clarity but the module can derive.\n\nActually let me keep it explicit and small: each case has `id`, `kind`, `expect`, `trials` (array of outcome strings).\n\nOutcomes: `allow`, `reject`, `timeout`, `crash`, `skip`, `missing`.\n\ncomplete = trials.length == k AND every trial outcome in {allow, reject}.\ntrialCorrect = outcome === expect.\ncaseAllPass = complete && all trials correct.\ncaseAnyPass = complete && some trial correct.\n\npass^k = #caseAllPass / #completeCases.\npass@k = #caseAnyPass / #completeCases.\n\nflake case = complete && trials[0] is incorrect (outcome !== expect, and it's a terminal allow/reject) && some later trial correct. => \"first trial failed, subsequent fixed rerun succeeded\". Wait \"fixed rerun\" implies the rerun is a re-run with same fixture deterministically. So just: trials[0] incorrect && some trial j>0 correct.\n\nflakeRate = #flakeCases / #completeCases.\n\nSecurity: security kind cases must have 0 skip and 0 flake. skip = any trial outcome === 'skip'. Also 0 flake for security.\n\nAnchor violations:\n- positive anchor: any positive case that is NOT all-pass → violation (but if positive case is incomplete, that's not an anchor \"fail\"? The criteria says positive must all pass; negative must all reject; violating → gate=fail). An incomplete positive case is not evidence of pass → should also be a violation? Hmm. Let me treat: positive case with a terminal incorrect trial → violation. Incomplete positive case → incomplete (excluded), but might also be flagged. The spec says \"正/负锚点：positive 必须全通过、negative 必须全部拒绝；违反即 gate=fail\". I'll define anchor violation strictly: a positive case that has any completed incorrect trial, OR is not all-pass among its completed cases... Simpler and faithful: \n  - positive violated if any positive case is complete but not all-pass, or has any terminal incorrect trial.\n  - negative violated if any negative/security case has any trial whose outcome is 'allow' (i.e., not rejected).\n  Actually \"negative 必须全部拒绝\" — every trial must reject. So any 'allow' outcome on a negative case → violation. And incomplete negative (skip/timeout) → missing evidence. For security there's the 0-skip rule.\n\nLet me define:\n- anchorViolations.positive = positive cases where caseAllPass is false (i.e., complete-but-not-all-pass, or incomplete). Hmm incomplete includes missing. But then a single missing positive case fails the gate. Is that desired? The criteria item 5 says insufficient sample → gate=insufficient-sample (not fail). And item 3 says security must have 0 skip/0 flake. I think it's cleaner:\n  - positiveViolations = positive cases that are complete and not all-pass → these are definite anchor failures.\n  - For incomplete positive cases: recorded as incomplete; do NOT silently pass. They reduce sample. But per item 4 \"positive 必须全通过 ... 违反即 gate=fail\". An incomplete positive is not \"on record as passing\". I'll flag incomplete positive/negative cases as an anchor \"evidence gap\" that yields gate=fail as well, because a positive anchor that did not run cannot be claimed to pass. Hmm, but the security/anchor semantics...\n\nLet me be pragmatic and deterministic, and document the rule clearly in the rule spec and acceptance:\n  - gate = 'fail' if any anchor violation: a positive case not all-pass among completed trials, or a negative/security case with any allowed trial; OR an anchor (positive/negative/security) case that is incomplete (missing evidence), since anchors are required to be fully present.\n  \nHmm, that may double-count with security skip budget. Actually I think it's cleaner and defensible to:\n  - positiveAnchors: must be complete + all-pass. Any positive case failing these (incomplete OR not-all-pass) → anchor violation.\n  - negativeAnchors: must be complete + all-reject. Any negative/security case failing (incomplete OR any allow) → anchor violation.\n  - security: additionally 0 skip, 0 flake.\n\nBut then item 5's \"insufficient-sample\" only triggers when overall complete case count < minCases. If anchors are all present and there are 20+ complete non-anchor cases... fine.\n\nLet me decide gate logic precedence:\n1. If any anchor violation OR security skip/flake violation OR non-security flake rate > max → gate='fail'.\n2. Else if completeCases < minCases → gate='insufficient-sample'.\n3. Else gate='pass'.\n\nHmm, order: should insufficient-sample beat fail? If we only have 5 cases and one positive anchor fails, gate should be fail (an actual violation). I'll put violations first (fail), then insufficient-sample. That's more conservative. Criteria item 5 says \"完整 case 数低于设计门（k=3、>=20）时 gate=insufficient-sample，绝不以小样本冒充 PASS\". So insufficient-sample supersedes PASS, not supersedes FAIL. Good: fail takes precedence over insufficient-sample.\n\nActually careful: \"绝不以小样本冒充 PASS\" - so we must not output pass with small sample. fail vs insufficient-sample both non-pass. Fine.\n\nLet me also compute per-trial success rate: over complete cases, across all trials, fraction of correct trials. And maybe per-trial position rates. I'll provide `trialSuccessRate` = correctTrials / totalTrials (over completed trials only). And also keep `incomplete` breakdown by reason.\n\nReport fields:\n```\nreliability: {\n  oracle: 'deterministic-fixture',\n  fixture: 'shared/agent-dev/reliability-fixture.json',\n  preRegistered: { k, minCases, nonSecurityFlakeRateMax, securitySkipBudget, securityFlakeBudget },\n  denominator: { cases: total, complete: n, incomplete: n },\n  incomplete: { total, byReason: {timeout, crash, skip, missing} , cases: [ids] },\n  passK: { count, rate },\n  passAtK: { count, rate },\n  trialSuccessRate: { correctTrials, completedTrials, rate },\n  flake: { count, rate, limit, cases: [ids] },\n  anchors: { positive: {...}, negative: {...}, violations: [...] },\n  security: { cases, skipCount, flakeCount, violations: [...] },\n  gate: 'pass'|'fail'|'insufficient-sample',\n  reasons: [...]\n}\n```\n\nNow integrate into agent-dev-benefit-eval.mjs: read the fixture, compute reliability, add to report and markdown. Keep existing fields intact.\n\nLet me also handle the \"not new metric platform\" requirement: reuse existing agent-dev benefit report + scenario registry. The module is a narrow aggregation lib reused by the benefit report. Good.\n\nNow module API. I'll export:\n- `RELIABILITY_OUTCOMES`\n- `INCOMPLETE_OUTCOMES`\n- `aggregateReliability(fixture, options)` → returns the reliability block.\n- `renderReliabilityMarkdown(reliability)` maybe.\n- `validateFixture(fixture)` → errors.\n\nAnd `computeGate`.\n\nNow the `.d.mts` file — follow the convention: `/* Auto-generated test typing surface for reliability-aggregation.mjs */` + `export const X: any;`. Let me see a fuller example with functions.\n\nLet me check an existing d.mts with functions.\n\nLet me look at scripts/lib/system-maturity-traceability.d.mts.\n\nI'll check that, then write everything. Let me also decide the test file environment: node.\n\nLet me look at how a test imports a scripts/lib mjs and asserts.\n\nNow, about harness validate: the task spec must be valid. Let me model it on harness-gap-closure-plan.md (scenario-less). It needs: lifecycleStatus, id, title, intent, touchedAreas, ownedPaths, requiredProfiles, requiredRules, humanEvidence, requiredTests, acceptance, expectedUserBehavior, docs. Not scenario/taskType required unless gateway/plugin.\n\nWait — validateTaskSpec requires fields id/title/scenario/taskType/intent but that's only for gateway/plugin tasks. For scenario-less specs, cli.mjs only checks `!spec.data.id || !spec.data.title`. Plus validateSpecPortability + validateChangedPathOwnership. And spec-lifecycle requires lifecycleStatus.\n\nSo the scenario-less task spec just needs id, title, lifecycleStatus for CLI validate to pass; but the source-inventory style includes richer fields. I'll include the richer fields to match repo conventions (touchedAreas, ownedPaths, requiredProfiles, requiredRules, humanEvidence, requiredTests, acceptance, expectedUserBehavior, docs).\n\nThe requiredRules I reference must exist as rule specs: `reliability-aggregation-boundary` (I create), plus maybe `docs-sync`, `task-forest-autoclose-and-roles`, `repository-artifact-retention`. requiredTests must be existing or new literal test paths/commands. And `validateSpecPortability` must not find machine-local paths.\n\nAlso note check-repository-artifacts.mjs: it scans task spec requiredTests for retired tests and `spec.docs.files` must be tracked non-local. My docs.files should be the closure md. But repository-artifacts check requires it be tracked and not local — since we can't git-add (no git), that check will fail anyway (no git). It's fine; not in the required checks we can run.\n\nHold on: the acceptance criteria mention \"harness validate/dry-run\" pass. Let me run `harness validate --spec ... --no-diff` (since no git). And `harness run --spec ... --dry-run --no-diff`. Let me check whether `--no-diff` works for run. In run(), validate is called with noDiff, changedFiles empty, scanFiles = touchedAreas; boundary scan runs on touchedAreas (my files are scripts/shared/tests/docs — src/ only is scanned, so fine). Then if no failures, selects steps and dry-run marks them skipped. Good.\n\nNow, requiredTests: harness run executes selectSteps(profiles). profiles from requiredProfiles. If requiredProfiles=[fast], selectSteps('fast') → some commands. With --dry-run they aren't run. So dry-run won't execute anything heavy. Good.\n\nLet me check profiles.mjs to see what fast includes (to ensure dry-run won't try to run).\n\nNow let me also decide about the fixture being \"deterministic oracle\". I'll design ~24 cases: 20 complete + a few incomplete, including positive anchors, negative anchors, security cases, and one flake case (for the reported rate, within limit), and one... hmm. Wait: security must have 0 flake. And non-security flake ≤ 1%. With ~20 complete cases, 1 flake case = 5% > 1% → gate fail. So I must NOT have flake in the main fixture, OR set flake limit appropriately. The criteria: \"非安全flake≤1%\" and \"flake = 首次 trial 失败、后续固定重跑成功的 case 比例\". With minCases=20, 1 flake = 0.05 > 0.01 → fail. So for a passing fixture, 0 flake cases. But then the report shows flake rate 0.00. That's fine and honest.\n\nBut to demonstrate the flake logic, the unit test can use synthetic inline fixtures (small) to show the flake branch. The tracked fixture can be a clean set that yields gate=pass (or insufficient-sample?).\n\nHmm. Let me think about what gate the tracked fixture should produce. Design gate k=3, minCases>=20. If fixture has exactly 20 complete cases (+ a few incomplete), complete=20 >= 20 → passes the sample gate. Anchors all pass. flake=0. → gate=pass. That demonstrates PASS honestly with synthetic oracle.\n\nBut wait — is that \"冒充 PASS\"? No: it's a synthetic oracle gate for the eval harness itself, explicitly labeled deterministic-fixture. It's fine as long as we don't claim real-model generalization.\n\nActually, I realize a subtlety: The fixture is a \"deterministic oracle\". Its purpose is to prove the aggregation logic, not to evaluate a real system. So a passing fixture is fine as long as it's labeled.\n\nLet me make the fixture: 24 cases total.\n- 3 positive anchor cases (all allow x3).\n- 2 negative anchor cases (all reject x3).\n- 2 security cases (all reject x3, no skip).\n- 13 more positive cases (all allow x3).\n- Incomplete cases: a few with timeout/crash/skip/missing to be excluded & preserved. But skip on a security case must be 0 — so put skip only on a non-security case.\n\n13+3 = 16 positive complete, +2 negative +2 security = 20 complete. Plus 4 incomplete (timeout, crash, skip, missing) → too many? That gives 20 complete exactly and 24 total. But hold on: security kind with skip → violation. I'll put skip on a positive (non-security) case → allowed (incomplete). But the criteria \"安全必测项 0 skip/0 flake\" implies other kinds may skip.\n\nHmm, but wait: if I add a case with first trial fail then later pass (flake) to show flake>0 → fail. Don't want that in tracked fixture. Keep 0 flake.\n\nLet me instead make complete=22 (>=20) and incomplete=4 to have margin. Let me just do:\npositive complete: 15\nnegative complete: 3\nsecurity complete: 3\n=> 21 complete\nincomplete (non-security): 1 timeout, 1 crash, 1 skip, 1 missing → 4 incomplete\ntotal = 25.\n\ncomplete=21 >= 20. anchors all pass. flake 0. gate=pass.\n\nGood. Let me write the fixture accordingly with distinct ids.\n\nNow the \"reasons\" array for gate. For pass: reasons e.g. [\"complete-cases>=minCases\", \"positive-anchors-all-pass\", \"negative-anchors-all-rejected\", \"security-skip=0\", \"security-flake=0\", \"non-security-flake<=limit\"]. For insufficient-sample: reason. For fail: the specific violations.\n\nNow let me define the module carefully and write tests.\n\nLet me also double-check: The task says don't create loops/retries. The `flake` concept involves \"rerun\" but that's data, not our code looping. Fine — I won't add retry logic in code.\n\nLet me write files now. First check profiles.mjs quickly and a d.mts with functions."}}
