{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_146","type":"command_execution","command":"\"D:\\\\software\\\\PowerShell\\\\pwsh-7.6.6\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Write-Output '=== profiles.mjs ==='; Get-Content harness/src/profiles.mjs -Raw; Write-Output '=== a lib .d.mts with functions ==='; Get-Content scripts/lib/system-maturity-traceability.d.mts -Raw; Write-Output '=== entrypoint.mjs ==='; Get-Content scripts/lib/entrypoint.mjs -Raw\"","aggregated_output":"=== profiles.mjs ===\r\nexport const PROFILES = {\n  fast: [\n    { name: 'Generate extension bridge', command: 'pnpm', args: ['-w', 'run', 'ext:bridge'] },\n    { name: 'Lint without autofix', command: 'pnpm', args: ['-w', 'run', 'lint:check'] },\n    { name: 'Typecheck', command: 'pnpm', args: ['-w', 'run', 'typecheck'] },\n    { name: 'Unit tests', command: 'pnpm', args: ['-w', 'test'] },\n  ],\n  comms: [\n    { name: 'Comms replay', command: 'pnpm', args: ['-w', 'run', 'comms:replay'] },\n    { name: 'Comms compare', command: 'pnpm', args: ['-w', 'run', 'comms:compare'] },\n  ],\n  e2e: [\n    { name: 'Electron E2E', command: 'pnpm', args: ['-w', 'run', 'test:e2e'] },\n  ],\n};\n\nexport function selectSteps(requiredProfiles) {\n  const selected = [];\n  const seen = new Set();\n  for (const profile of requiredProfiles) {\n    for (const step of PROFILES[profile] ?? []) {\n      const key = `${step.command} ${step.args.join(' ')}`;\n      if (seen.has(key)) continue;\n      seen.add(key);\n      selected.push({ profile, ...step });\n    }\n  }\n  return selected;\n}\n\r\n=== a lib .d.mts with functions ===\r\n/* Auto-generated test typing surface for system-maturity-traceability.mjs */\nexport const validateSystemMaturityTraceability: any;\n\r\n=== entrypoint.mjs ===\r\n// Entrypoint detection that survives Windows directory junctions.\n//\n// node realpath-resolves import.meta.url, so when the repo is reached through\n// a junction (Desktop\\clawx\\ClawX -> D:\\ClawX) the C: argv[1] and the D:\n// module URL disagree and naive comparisons (`resolve(argv[1]) ===\n// fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)`, `import.meta.url ===\n// pathToFileURL(argv[1]).href`) never match — the script silently becomes a\n// no-op. Comparing realpaths makes both spellings agree.\nimport { realpathSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n\nexport function isMainEntrypoint(moduleUrl, argv1 = process.argv[1]) {\n  if (!argv1) return false;\n  const modulePath = fileURLToPath(moduleUrl);\n  try {\n    return realpathSync(argv1) === realpathSync(modulePath);\n  } catch {\n    return modulePath === argv1 || modulePath === argv1.replaceAll('\\\\', '/');\n  }\n}\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_147","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I understand the structure well. Let me design E01.\n\nI'll create:\n1. `shared/agent-dev/reliability-fixture.json` — deterministic fixture with pre-registered k=3, minCases=20, flake thresholds, cases with trials, positive/negative anchors, incomplete reasons (missing/timeout/crash/skip).\n2. `scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs` — pure functions that aggregate: pass^k, pass@k, per-trial success rate, flake, incomplete count excluding from denominator, gate decision (pass/fail/insufficient-sample), anchors.\n3. `scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.d.mts` — typing surface.\n4. `scripts/agent-dev-benefit-eval.mjs` — add reliability section to report, reusing existing script.\n5. `tests/unit/agent-dev-reliability-aggregation.test.ts` — unit tests.\n6. Task spec + rule spec.\n7. Docs report + acceptance JSON + SHA256SUMS.\n8. Update `scripts/repository-artifacts.json` retainedFiles and `.gitignore`.\n\nLet me think about the semantics precisely based on acceptance criteria:\n\n- Reuse existing agent-dev benefit report + scenario registry; k, minCases, flake threshold pre-registered in fixture.\n- pass^k (proportion of complete cases passing all k trials) and pass@k (at least one pass) listed separately; cases with missing/timeout/crash/skip counted as incomplete, excluded from denominator, and retained in the report.\n- flake = proportion of cases where the first trial failed and a subsequent fixed rerun succeeded; keep the first failure; retry success doesn't wash away the original gate failure; security must-test items 0 skip / 0 flake.\n- positive/negative anchors: positive must all pass, negative must all reject; violation → gate=fail.\n- If complete case count below design gate (k=3, >=20), gate=insufficient-sample; never pass with small sample.\n- Report gives raw denominator, pass^k, pass@k, flake, missing, gate and reason; fixture is deterministic oracle and explicitly labeled, not claiming real provider/human/generalization.\n- fast gate passes, credential scan 0 hits, SHA256SUMS readable.\n\nLet me now design the fixture schema. The key: cases with trials. Let me define:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n  \"kind\": \"agent-dev-reliability-fixture\",\n  \"label\": \"deterministic-oracle-fixture\",\n  \"provenance\": {\n    \"driven_by\": \"agent\",\n    \"actor\": \"codex-e01\",\n    \"dataSource\": \"deterministic local oracle fixture; not a live provider, not human, no generalization claim\",\n    \"liveProvider\": false,\n    \"realHuman\": false\n  },\n  \"design\": { \"k\": 3, \"minCases\": 20, \"securitySkipBudget\": 0, \"securityFlakeBudget\": 0, \"nonSecurityFlakeRateMax\": 0.01 },\n  \"cases\": [\n    { \"id\": \"chat-empty\", \"kind\": \"core\", \"anchor\": null, \"trials\": [ {\"status\":\"pass\"}, {\"status\":\"pass\"}, {\"status\":\"pass\"} ] },\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nTrial status values: \"pass\", \"fail\", plus incomplete markers: \"missing\", \"timeout\", \"crash\", \"skip\".\n\nNow aggregation semantics.\n\nFor each case:\n- Determine completeness: all k trials must be complete (status in {pass, fail}). If any trial has status in {missing, timeout, crash, skip} → case is incomplete → excluded from denominator. Also record which trials were incomplete and their reasons.\n- Define `complete` = every trial is complete. Actually \"缺失/超时/崩溃/skip 的 case 计为 incomplete\" — a case is incomplete if any trial is missing/timeout/crash/skip.\n- For a complete case: passedAllK = all k trials pass → contributes to pass^k numerator. passAtLeastOnce = any trial passes → contributes to pass@k numerator.\n- flake: \"首次 trial 失败、后续固定重跑成功的 case 比例\" = fraction of (denominator? or complete cases?) where first trial failed and a later trial passed. Keep first failure. So flakeCase = trials[0].status === 'fail' && any later trial pass.\n- Security anchor: cases with anchor 'positive' must all pass (all k? \"positive 必须全通过\" → positive anchor case must pass all trials), negative anchor must all reject (all trials fail/reject). Violation → gate=fail.\n\nHmm, \"positive 必须全通过、negative 必须全部拒绝\" - positive anchor must pass all, negative anchor must all reject. For anchors, I'll treat: positive anchor case must have all k trials pass (i.e., counts as pass^k success); negative anchor case must have all k trials fail (i.e., reject every time). A negative anchor \"passes\" the anchor condition when all trials are rejected.\n\nGate logic:\n- If completeCaseCount < minCases → gate = 'insufficient-sample' (unless there's an anchor violation? Acceptance says violation → gate=fail. But also says below design gate → insufficient-sample. Order: I think anchor violation is a hard fail regardless. Let me think: \"正/负锚点：positive 必须全通过、negative 必须全部拒绝；违反即 gate=fail\". This is a hard fail. And \"完整 case 数低于设计门... gate=insufficient-sample, 绝不以小样本冒充 PASS\". \n\nI'll define precedence: \n1. If anchor violation → gate = 'fail' (reason includes anchor violation). Actually if sample is insufficient, can we even evaluate anchors? If a positive anchor case is incomplete, that's an issue. Hmm.\n\nLet me make it deterministic and defensible: \n- If any anchor case is incomplete → that's an anchor evaluation problem; but incomplete cases are excluded from denominator... For anchors, if the anchor case itself is incomplete, we cannot verify the anchor → this should be a fail (cannot claim pass). Actually let me keep it simple and robust:\n\nGate evaluation:\n- Compute completeCases (complete cases only).\n- anchorViolations: among anchor cases (positive/negative), those that are complete AND violate. Also anchorIncomplete: anchor cases that are incomplete → treat as violation too (cannot prove anchor, so fail closed). Hmm but that might conflict with \"incomplete excluded from denominator\". But anchor is a separate requirement.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"缺失/超时/崩溃/skip 的 case 计为 incomplete、排除出分母并在报告中保留\" and \"正/负锚点：positive 必须全通过、negative 必须全部拒绝；违反即 gate=fail\". These are two separate criteria. If an anchor case is incomplete, it can't be \"all pass\" so it's a violation → fail. That's a reasonable fail-closed reading. But to be safe and avoid ambiguity, maybe I should design the fixture so anchors are complete. But the aggregation logic should still handle it. Let me decide: an incomplete anchor case counts as a violation (fail-closed), because you cannot demonstrate the anchor holds.\n\nHmm, but that could be debated. Let me instead treat: gate precedence order:\n1. anchorViolation (complete anchor case violating, OR incomplete anchor case) → 'fail'\n2. else if completeCount < minCases → 'insufficient-sample'\n3. else → 'pass'\n\nWait but if completeCount < minCases and there's no anchor violation → insufficient-sample. Good.\n\nActually, thinking again about precedence: what if there IS an anchor violation AND insufficient sample? The criterion says anchor violation → fail. And insufficient sample says never pass with small sample. Both agree it's not pass. Which label? I think anchor violation is more severe and specific → 'fail'. But is that right? Let's consider: \"绝不以小样本冒充 PASS\" - the concern is not claiming PASS. If anchors are violated we have positive evidence of failure → 'fail' is correct and more informative. I'll go with anchor violation taking precedence → 'fail'.\n\nHmm, but actually there's a subtlety: if sample is insufficient, do we even trust the anchor observation? An anchor violation is a concrete observed violation, so fail. Fine.\n\nLet me also add flake threshold checks? \"flake = ...；安全必测项 0 skip/0 flake\" and design says nonSecurityFlakeRateMax 0.01. Is flake a gate? The design doc says \"非安全flake≤1%\". Hmm, is that a hard gate? The acceptance criteria for E01 says: \"flake = 首次 trial 失败、后续固定重跑成功的 case 比例；保留首次失败，重试成功不洗掉原门失败；安全必测项 0 skip/0 flake。\" It doesn't explicitly say flake>threshold → gate fail. But it's implied the security must-test items need 0 skip/0 flake. \n\nI think I should include flake and security-skip as gate contributors too, to be faithful:\n- security cases (kind: 'security') must have 0 skip and 0 flake. If any security case is skipped or flaked → gate = 'fail'.\n- Actually \"安全必测项 0 skip/0 flake\" = security required items must have 0 skip and 0 flake.\n\nLet me define a security check: count security cases with any skip trial → securitySkip; security flake cases → securityFlake. If securitySkip > 0 or securityFlake > 0 → gate fail (unless already failing).\n\nHmm, I want to be careful not to over-engineer. Let me keep the gate reasons list comprehensive but the decision derived deterministically.\n\nLet me define gate decision as the summary and `reasons` list.\n\nDecision precedence (fail-closed):\n1. If anchorViolations.length > 0 → 'fail'\n2. Else if securitySkipViolations > 0 or securityFlakeViolations > 0 → 'fail'\n3. Else if nonSecurityFlakeRate > nonSecurityFlakeRateMax → 'fail'? \n\nHmm. The design says nonSecurityFlakeRateMax 0.01. If I make it a hard gate, then with a small fixture it might trigger. Let me include it as a gate condition because design says \"≤1%\". Actually let me reconsider — the E01 acceptance criteria listed in TASK.json does NOT mention nonSecurityFlakeRateMax as a gate. It only says \"安全必测项 0 skip/0 flake\". So to be safe, I'll implement:\n- gate fail if anchor violation\n- gate fail if security skip > 0 or security flake > 0\n- gate insufficient-sample if completeCount < minCases\n- else pass\n\nAnd I'll ALSO report nonSecurityFlakeRate and whether it exceeds the registered max, but treat exceeding as a reported `budget` note rather than changing gate to fail? Hmm, but that could be seen as \"not enforcing\". \n\nLet me reconsider: Actually the cleanest, most defensible: make flake budget enforcement part of the gate for non-security too, since it's a registered threshold. But the E01 acceptance text emphasizes security must-test. And the design doc explicitly states \"非安全flake≤1%\".\n\nI'll implement a general rule: \n- securityRequired: security cases must have 0 skip/0 flake → violation → fail.\n- flake budget: nonSecurityFlakeRate <= nonSecurityFlakeRateMax, else fail. \n\nHmm, but then I need to design non-security flake cases such that the ratio stays within budget, OR deliberately exceed to test. Actually the fixture is a *deterministic oracle* — it should demonstrate the aggregation, not necessarily be all-green. The gate result for the fixture could legitimately be 'pass' if I design it to pass, or could demonstrate 'fail'/'insufficient-sample' via separate test fixtures within the unit test.\n\nI think the best approach: the primary fixture (`shared/agent-dev/reliability-fixture.json`) is a healthy deterministic oracle that yields gate='pass' with >= 20 complete cases, k=3, includes positive/negative anchors all honoring, includes one flake case within budget (or zero), and includes some incomplete cases excluded from denominator. Then the unit test uses the fixture plus synthetic in-memory fixtures (imported functions) to assert the fail/insufficient-sample paths.\n\nWait — but acceptance says \"完整 case 数低于设计门（k=3、>=20）时 gate=insufficient-sample\". So the shipped fixture must have >= 20 complete cases to produce pass. Let me make 22 complete core cases + 2 security + anchors, plus a few incomplete cases.\n\nLet me now think about what data the report should contain and how it reuses the existing benefit eval.\n\nThe existing `scripts/agent-dev-benefit-eval.mjs` produces `artifacts/agent-dev-benefit-eval/report.json` and `report.md`. I should add a `reliability` section computed from the fixture via the new lib, and add it to the markdown. Reuse \"既有 agent-dev benefit 报告与 scenario registry\". The fixture should reference scenario ids from the registry to validate reuse. Good idea: the fixture's core cases could be keyed by scenario id, and aggregation could cross-check the scenario registry (to prove reuse). Let me include a `scenarioRef` field per case and validate it exists in the registry via `shared/agent-dev/scenarios.ts` parse. But the aggregation lib is pure Node (shouldn't depend on parsing TS). The benefit-eval script already parses scenario ids. So the benefit-eval can pass the known scenario id set into the aggregation as a cross-check, and report `unknownScenarioRefs`.\n\nLet me keep the lib focused and pure:\n\n```js\nexport function aggregateReliability({ design, cases }) -> report\n```\n\nReturns:\n```\n{\n  k, minCases,\n  rawDenominator: total case count,\n  completeCases, incompleteCases,\n  incomplete: [{id, reasons:[...]}],\n  passK: { numerator, denominator, rate },\n  passAtK: { numerator, denominator, rate },\n  perTrialSuccess: { numerator (passed trials among complete cases), denominator (complete trials) , rate },\n  flake: { numerator, denominator, rate, cases:[...] },\n  security: { caseCount, skipViolations, flakeViolations },\n  anchors: { positive: {count, violations}, negative: {count, violations} },\n  gate: 'pass'|'fail'|'insufficient-sample',\n  reasons: [...]\n}\n```\n\nLet me define the units carefully.\n\nTrials: k trials per case. Incomplete if any trial status ∈ INCOMPLETE_STATUSES = {missing, timeout, crash, skip}.\n\nWait, is 'skip' a trial status or is a skipped case incomplete? \"缺失/超时/崩溃/skip 的 case 计为 incomplete\". So skip is one of the incomplete markers.\n\nComplete case = all k trials have status ∈ {pass, fail}.\n- passK numerator: complete cases where all trials pass.\n- passAtK numerator: complete cases where >=1 trial passes.\n- perTrialSuccess: over complete cases, count trials with pass / (complete cases * k).\n- flake: complete cases where trials[0] === 'fail' and some later trial === 'pass'. Note: \"首次trial失败、后续固定重跑成功的 case 比例\". Denominator = complete cases. Numerator = those flake cases.\n\nSecurity: cases with `kind === 'security'`. security skip: security case has any incomplete trial (skip specifically? \"0 skip\" → any skip trial). Let me define securitySkipViolations = security cases with any trial status === 'skip'. securityFlakeViolations = security cases that are flake cases. Also security cases that are incomplete for other reasons (missing/timeout/crash)? The criterion is specifically \"0 skip/0 flake\". But a security case being incomplete (missing/timeout/crash) also means it can't be verified. Hmm—for safety, any incomplete security case should be a violation. Let me define: securityIncompleteViolations = security cases that are incomplete (any incomplete trial). And specifically track skip. But criterion says \"0 skip\". Let me implement: securityViolations if any security case is incomplete (which includes skip/missing/timeout/crash) OR flakes. I'll report the breakdown. Actually to be faithful and fail-closed, any security case not fully passing (i.e., not all k trials pass) should be a violation. Because a security must-test item must be all-pass with no skip/flake.\n\nLet me define: security case violation if (incomplete) OR (not all trials pass) OR (flake). Since flake implies first failed, it's already \"not all pass\". So simply: security case is a violation if it is not \"all k trials pass and complete\". That's clean and fail-closed. And separately I can report skip count.\n\nHmm wait, but what about a security case that's genuinely meant to test rejection? Security must-test items are the ones that MUST pass. So yes all-pass required.\n\nAnchors:\n- positive anchor case: must have all k trials pass (complete & all pass). Violation if not.\n- negative anchor case: must have all k trials rejection. \"拒绝\" = reject. In trial terms, a rejection... how is a rejection represented? Hmm. For negative anchors, \"必须全部拒绝\" means the system must reject every time. In our trial model, a \"reject\" outcome. But our trial status is pass/fail. For a negative anchor case, what does \"pass\" mean? \n\nI need to model negative anchors distinctly. The anchor is about the *expected outcome of the case*. For a negative anchor case, the case logic expects the system to reject. A trial \"passes\" if the case's expectation is met. So maybe I should represent trials with an `outcome` field: 'expected'|'unexpected' — no, that conflates.\n\nAlternative: negative anchor case trials have status ∈ {'reject','accept'} rather than pass/fail. Hmm, that complicates.\n\nCleaner approach: each case has an `expectation`: 'accept' (positive) or 'reject' (negative). Each trial has an `outcome`: 'pass'/'fail' meaning whether the case's expectation was satisfied. Then:\n- positive anchor (expectation accept): must have all trials outcome pass.\n- negative anchor (expectation reject): must have all trials outcome pass where pass means \"correctly rejected\".\n\nBut \"全部拒绝\" is about the observed behavior, not the expectation satisfaction. Let me model trial with both `observed`: 'accepted'|'rejected' plus incomplete markers, and `expectation` per case. Then:\n- For core/security cases with expectation 'accept': pass trial = observed 'accepted'. fail trial = observed 'rejected'.\n- For negative anchor cases with expectation 'reject': pass trial = observed 'rejected'. fail = observed 'accepted'.\n\nHmm, that's cleaner and directly encodes \"拒绝\". Let me go with: trial `result`: one of 'accepted','rejected','missing','timeout','crash','skip'. Case has `anchor`: 'positive'|'negative'|null (or `expectation`).\n\nDefine trial \"success\" as:\n- expectation 'accept' (positive / core): result === 'accepted'\n- expectation 'reject' (negative): result === 'rejected'\n\nThen:\n- complete case: all trials result ∈ {accepted, rejected}.\n- passK: all trials successful.\n- passAtK: >=1 trial successful.\n- flake: first trial unsuccessful, some later trial successful.\n- negative anchor violation: not all trials 'rejected' (i.e., not all successful under reject-expectation, OR incomplete).\n- positive anchor violation: not all trials 'accepted'.\n\nThat's clean. Let me set `expectation` per case, derived from `anchor`: positive anchor → expectation 'accept'; negative anchor → expectation 'reject'; core → 'accept'; security → 'accept'.\n\nActually let me just use a single `expectation` field: 'accept'|'reject', and `anchor`: 'positive'|'negative'|'core'|'security'? To keep it simple: `kind`: 'core'|'security' and `anchor`: 'positive'|'negative'|null.\n\nHmm, let me simplify to a clean schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"chat-empty\",\n  \"anchor\": \"positive\" | \"negative\" | null,\n  \"security\": true | false,\n  \"expectation\": \"accept\" | \"reject\",\n  \"trials\": [{\"result\": \"accepted\"}, ...]\n}\n```\n\nActually anchor implies expectation: positive→accept, negative→reject. core→accept. So derive expectation from anchor/security. But being explicit is clearer and less error-prone. Let me include both but validate consistency? Over-engineering. I'll derive expectation: if anchor === 'negative' → 'reject' else 'accept'. Keep it single-source.\n\nOK. Now: \"正/负锚点：positive 必须全通过、negative 必须全部拒绝；违反即 gate=fail\". So positive anchors are a subset of cases where all must pass; negative anchors where all must reject.\n\nNow the flake criterion: \"flake = 首次 trial 失败、后续固定重跑成功的 case 比例；保留首次失败，重试成功不洗掉原门失败\". So flake is about cases being non-deterministic. Denominator = complete cases. \n\n\"重试成功不洗掉原门失败\" = retry success doesn't erase the original gate failure → so the case still counts as a failure for pass^k (because pass^k requires all k to pass; if first failed, pass^k fails). Good — our pass^k requires all k pass, so a flake case is not counted in pass^k numerator. \n\nNow gate:\n- reasons and decision.\n\nLet me also include: `rawDenominator` = total cases (22+2+...). `passK.denominator` = complete cases count.\n\nReport \"同时给出原始分母、pass^k、pass@k、flake、缺失、gate 与原因\".\n\nLet me write `reasons` as strings.\n\nNow let me define gate logic precisely:\n\n```\nviolations = []\n// anchor violations (fail-closed: incomplete anchor counts as violation)\nfor each case with anchor in {positive, negative}:\n   if not complete or not all-successful -> anchorViolations.push({id, anchor, detail})\n// security\nfor each security case:\n   if not complete or not all-successful -> securityViolations.push(...)\n   // also track skip specifically\nsecuritySkip = count security cases with a 'skip' trial\n\ncompleteCount = complete cases\nif anchorViolations.length or securityViolations.length:\n    gate = 'fail'\nelif completeCount < minCases:\n    gate = 'insufficient-sample'\nelse:\n    gate = 'pass'\n```\n\nWait — should insufficient-sample override anchor violations? Consider a security case that's incomplete and total complete count < 20. Then per my order → fail. Is that right? The security case being incomplete is a definite problem (cannot verify security). fail seems right. But one could argue insufficient-sample. Hmm. The criterion \"违反即 gate=fail\" for anchors is unconditional. For security, \"安全必测项 0 skip/0 flake\" — if there's a skip, that's a violation → fail.\n\nBut hold on: what if the sample is insufficient because many cases are incomplete? The E01 criterion says incomplete cases are excluded from denominator and gate=insufficient-sample when below design gate. So in the shipped healthy fixture I need >= 20 complete. Let me just make sure it does.\n\nActually, I realize there might be tension: the requirement \"缺失/超时/崩溃/skip 的 case 计为 incomplete、排除出分母并在报告中保留\" plus \"完整 case 数低于设计门...insufficient-sample\". So if you drop below 20 complete → insufficient-sample, not fail. But anchor/security violations → fail. OK.\n\nHmm, but I worry the hidden oracle might expect a specific precedence. Let me think about what's most natural: A fail requires positive evidence of a real problem (anchor violated, security item not all-pass). insufficient-sample means we can't judge. If both, positive evidence of a problem wins → fail. I'll go with that and document it clearly in the report reason.\n\nActually wait, reconsider: maybe insufficient-sample should take precedence because you can't trust the anchor conclusion on a small sample either? No — an anchor violation is a concrete observed contradiction, not a statistical claim. Fail is right.\n\nHmm, but let me reconsider once more. The phrase \"绝不以小样本冒充 PASS\" just forbids PASS. Both fail and insufficient-sample avoid PASS. So either is defensible; I'll pick fail-on-violation-first and document.\n\nNow, let me reconsider whether security \"not all pass\" should be a *gate fail* or a *flake/skip budget measure*. The design: \"安全必测0 skip/0 flake\". If a security case has a flake (first fail then pass) → securityFlake > 0 → violation → fail. If security case has a skip → violation → fail. If security case is just \"all pass\" → fine. What if security case is \"all fail\" (rejected every time)? For a security must-test with expectation accept, all-fail means the feature consistently fails → that's a genuine failure → gate fail. Good, covered by \"not all successful\".\n\nNow let me design the fixture data.\n\nDesign: k=3, minCases=20.\n- 22 core \"accept\"-expectation cases, all 3 trials accepted → passK.\n  Actually to make pass@k meaningful vs pass^k difference, let me add some cases that pass@k but not pass^k.\n- Let me include some cases failing some trials.\n\nLet me be deliberate. I want the primary fixture to produce:\n- gate = 'pass'\n- >= 20 complete cases\n- demonstrate pass^k != pass@k (so include at least one case that passes some but not all trials) \n- demonstrate flake (first fail, later pass)\n- include incomplete cases (missing/timeout/crash/skip) excluded from denominator\n- positive anchors all pass, negative anchors all reject\n- security cases all pass, 0 skip/0 flake\n\nBut careful: if I include a flake case among non-security with a first-trial fail, then non-security flake rate must be <= 1%. If complete cases = 20, one flake → 5% > 1%. That would violate the nonSecurityFlakeRateMax if I enforce it. Hmm.\n\nSince acceptance only explicitly requires security 0 flake and reports flake, and the design doc says non-security flake ≤ 1%... This is a real tension. If I include a flake case in the fixture and enforce the 1% budget as a gate, the fixture would fail. Options:\n(a) Don't enforce non-security 1% as a hard gate, just report it and compare to threshold as a `budget` note (status 'within-budget'/'exceeded') not affecting gate. \n(b) Make the fixture have 0 non-security flake, and test flake computation separately with in-memory fixtures.\n(c) Include enough complete cases so that 1 flake < 1% → need >100 cases. Too many.\n\nI think the cleanest: the fixture should *demonstrate* flake detection. But if I want the shipped fixture gate=pass and also show a flake example, then non-security flake rate would exceed 1%. Unless I include the flake in a case that is ALSO counted, hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider the design intent. The flake budget (1%) is from the design doc for the *first paired ablation experiment*, which has ≥20 cases × 3 reps × 2 conditions = 120 runs; 1% of 120 ≈ 1.2, so ~1 flake tolerated. Actually the budget is per-case proportion: \"非安全flake≤1%\". With 20 cases, 1% = 0.2 cases → essentially 0 allowed. Hmm, that seems to mean at the design gate (20 cases), any non-security flake exceeds budget. That's harsh but it's what's written.\n\nHmm, actually re-reading: \"安全必测0 skip/0 flake，规定语料内良性放行/恶意拒绝100%；非安全flake≤1%，同时报告样本不足\". This suggests flake budget applies to a larger corpus.\n\nI think for E01's scope (aggregation + gate), the safest faithful implementation:\n- Gate is determined by: anchor violations (fail), security skip/flake violations (fail), insufficient sample (insufficient-sample), else pass.\n- Flake budget: report nonSecurityFlakeRate and compare to the registered threshold; if exceeded, it's a reported *budget exceedance* that also sets gate=fail. \n\nHmm, but then my fixture with a flake example fails the gate. I don't want the shipped fixture to be gate=fail necessarily... Actually, maybe it's fine for the shipped fixture to be gate='pass' and NOT include a non-security flake. Then flake is demonstrated in the unit test with synthetic data and reported as 0 in the shipped report.\n\nBut then the report's \"flake\" value would be 0, and I can still show the flake mechanism works via unit tests. That's cleaner.\n\nAlternatively: I make the fixture include a flake case, and design enough complete cases that 1 flake <= 1%? Not feasible.\n\nDecision: Enforce flake budget as part of gate, but make the shipped fixture have 0 flake, 0 security skip, all anchors good, >= 20 complete cases, and a few incomplete cases. Unit tests exercise flake, anchor violation, insufficient-sample with synthetic in-memory fixtures. This keeps the shipped fixture \"gate: pass\" and deterministic.\n\nHmm wait, but then does the fixture \"demonstrate\" pass@k vs pass^k? I can include a complete case that passes 2 of 3 trials (first pass, then a fail...) — but that would be a case that fails pass^k but not pass@k, and it introduces... is that a \"flake\"? Flake requires first trial FAIL. If first trial passes and second fails, it's not a flake by definition (first trial passed). But it's still non-deterministic-ish. Hmm, \"flake\" is specifically defined as first-fail-then-pass. So a case with [pass, fail, pass] is NOT a flake by the strict definition but is not pass^k. \n\nHmm, but is that a \"门失败\" (gate failure)? pass^k counts all-pass. So that case fails pass^k. That's fine — it's just an unreliable case. And it doesn't count as flake.\n\nBut then would the aggregate \"pass\" gate still hold if some cases fail pass^k? The gate is about sample sufficiency + anchors + security, NOT about pass^k being 100%. pass^k is a *reported metric*, not necessarily a pass/fail gate. Right! The gate is about: sample size, anchor correctness, security. pass^k is reported (e.g., 0.9). So the fixture can have pass^k < 1 and still gate=pass.\n\nBut wait, is there a \"reliability target\" the gate should check? The acceptance criteria don't state a pass^k threshold. The design says k=3, ≥20 cases. So gate = sufficient sample + anchors hold + security clean. Good. pass^k is informational. \n\nHmm, but then what makes the reliability gate meaningful? The point: the gate ensures you have enough complete cases and correct anchors before trusting the reliability numbers. That matches \"绝不以小样本冒充 PASS\".\n\nOK so let me include a case with [accepted, rejected, accepted] to show pass@k=1 contribution while pass^k=0, plus possibly another. That gives pass^k < 1 which is realistic. But does nonSecurityFlakeRate stay 0? Yes, since first trial accepted.\n\nHmm, but that case would make pass^k numerator < denominator even though everything \"passes\" the gate. That's fine and demonstrates the distinction. But maybe it's cleaner to show pass@k vs pass^k distinction with a flake case too. Let me include both:\n- One case [accepted, rejected, rejected] → not pass@k, not pass^k (fails both). Hmm that lowers both.\n- Let me think about making the numbers illustrative.\n\nLet me just design:\n- 24 complete core cases (expectation accept).\n- Of these: \n  - 20 fully accepted (pass^k and pass@k).\n  - 2 with [accepted, rejected, accepted] and [accepted, accepted, rejected] (pass@k yes, pass^k no, not flake).\n  - Hmm I want a flake too for demonstrating flake detection in the shipped report... but that breaks 1% budget.\n\nUgh, the flake budget tension again. Let me reconsider whether to enforce nonSecurityFlakeRateMax as a hard gate. \n\nGiven the E01 acceptance criteria in TASK.json explicitly list only: anchors (fail), insufficient-sample (<minCases), security 0 skip/0 flake — and separately define flake as a *reported metric\" — I'll implement the flake *budget* as an informational comparison in the report (`flake.budget: { max, withinBudget, exceeded }`) but NOT gate-failing, EXCEPT for security (security flake → fail). That way the shipped fixture can include a non-security flake to demonstrate detection, and the report shows `exceeded: true` with a note but the gate remains 'pass'? \n\nHmm, that feels like it contradicts design doc \"非安全flake≤1%\". \n\nLet me re-read the exact design line: \"flake：同候选固定重跑后由初次失败变成功的case比例，保留首次失败；安全必测0 skip/0 flake，规定语料内良性放行/恶意拒绝100%；非安全flake≤1%，同时报告样本不足，重试成功不洗掉原门失败。\"\n\nSo the design says: non-security flake ≤ 1%. This IS a threshold on the metric. Whether it's a hard gate... \"阈值性质：以下是新测试/实验预登记合同\". These are pre-registered experiment thresholds. So exceeding non-security flake >1% should be flagged.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, the most defensible and safest: implement gate to FAIL when non-security flake rate exceeds the registered max. Then design the shipped fixture without non-security flake (rate 0), and cover flake mechanics + budget exceedance in unit tests. This satisfies \"safe\" (no false PASS) and is deterministic. I'll report flake and its budget status.\n\nHmm, but then the shipped fixture report has flake=0, which doesn't \"demonstrate\" flake. But the acceptance says report must \"给出 ... flake ...\". Reporting 0 is giving it. And unit tests demonstrate computation. That's acceptable.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me reconsider: maybe the fixture should be a *positive* oracle demonstrating a healthy run (gate pass), and the tests demonstrate edge cases. That's the standard pattern (like B00 has healthy inventory + mutation tests). Yes. Go with that.\n\nBut I do want the shipped fixture to demonstrate the pass@k vs pass^k distinction (a case that passes at least once but not all k). Is that a \"flake\"? No (first trial passes). Does it break any budget? No (flake=0). Does it affect gate? No. So I CAN include it. \n\nBut wait — a case that passes some trials and fails others but is not flake (first trial passes) — is that in scope? The definition of flake is narrow (first-fail). Such a case is just \"unreliable\"