{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_150","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have a good understanding. Let me design the implementation.\n\nDesign:\n1. `shared/agent-dev/reliability-fixture.json` - deterministic oracle fixture with pre-registered k=3, minCases=20, flake thresholds, security budgets, and case trial records.\n2. `scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs` + `.d.mts` - pure aggregation logic.\n3. `scripts/agent-dev-benefit-eval.mjs` - integrate reliability aggregation into the report.\n4. `tests/unit/agent-dev-reliability-aggregation.test.ts` - unit test.\n5. `harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-reliability-aggregation.md` - task spec (with lifecycleStatus).\n6. `harness/specs/rules/reliability-aggregation-boundary.md` - rule spec.\n7. `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/e01-reliability-aggregation.md` - report.\n8. `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/e01-acceptance.json` - acceptance record.\n9. `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/E01-SHA256SUMS` - digest manifest.\n10. Also update `scripts/repository-artifacts.json` retainedFiles and `.gitignore` reviewed-block? Wait — targetScope doesn't include those. The task targetScope is exactly 10 files. Let me re-read.\n\ntargetScope:\n- scripts/agent-dev-benefit-eval.mjs\n- scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs\n- scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.d.mts\n- shared/agent-dev/reliability-fixture.json\n- tests/unit/agent-dev-reliability-aggregation.test.ts\n- harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-reliability-aggregation.md\n- harness/specs/rules/reliability-aggregation-boundary.md\n- docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/e01-reliability-aggregation.md\n- docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/e01-acceptance.json\n- docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/E01-SHA256SUMS\n\nSo I should stick to these exactly. The repository-artifacts.json and .gitignore are NOT in scope — meaning the E01 acceptance files are under docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/ which is a reviewed directory with explicit retainedFiles allowlist plus the trailing `!/docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/` block. Hmm, actually the final block negates the directory but only re-includes specific files. New files e01-* would be ignored. But I shouldn't modify .gitignore since it's out of scope and there's no git anyway. Since there's no git, these checks can't run. I'll keep to scope.\n\nWait, but the acceptance criteria says \"SHA256SUMS 可回读\" (SHA256SUMS can be read back), and \"凭据扫描 0 命中\" (credential scan 0 hits). I'll build the SHA256SUMS covering the retained files and verify it with `verify-evidence-sha256sums.mjs`. But that script only verifies manifests in dirs that have INTEGRITY.json. E01-SHA256SUMS dir would not have INTEGRITY.json... Actually the harness-gap-closure dir has other SHA256SUMS files but no INTEGRITY.json. So `verifyAllManifests` won't pick them up. I can still verify read-back manually using `verifyManifest` style: write my own tiny verification in the acceptance, or just run `verify-evidence-sha256sums` and note it doesn't cover. Better: write my own read-back check inline in a node script during my validation run (not part of deliverable). And the acceptance record will include the digest results.\n\nLet me think about what \"design gate k=3, >=20\" means: pre-register in fixture: k=3, minCases=20, flake thresholds, security skip budget 0, security flake budget 0, nonSecurityFlakeRateMax 0.01.\n\nNow the aggregation module design. Requirements:\n\n- Input: case trial records. Each case has id, `security` boolean (safety must-test), and trials: array of outcomes. Each trial outcome ∈ {pass, fail, incomplete-reason: missing/timeout/crash/skip}.\n- pass^k := proportion of complete cases where ALL k trials passed. Denominator = complete cases only (cases with exactly k trials all present and none incomplete/missing/timeout/crash/skip). Incomplete cases excluded from denominator but retained in report.\n- pass@k := proportion of complete cases where at least one trial passed.\n- per-trial success rate: across all counted trials? \"逐 trial 成功率\" — success rate per trial.\n- flake := proportion of complete cases where first trial failed and later fixed re-run passes. \"flake = 首次 trial 失败、后续固定重跑成功的 case 比例；保留首次失败，重试成功不洗掉原门失败\". So flake counts cases where trial[0] = fail but some subsequent trial (re-run) = pass. Keep the first failure (don't overwrite). And flake must not wash out the gate failure - i.e., a flaky case still counts as fail for pass^k.\n- Incomplete cases: reason recorded (missing/timeout/crash/skip), counted as incomplete, excluded from denominator, retained in report.\n- Safety must-test: 0 skip/0 flake required. If any security case has a skip or is flaky → gate=fail.\n- positive/negative anchors: positive anchor cases must all pass; negative anchor cases must all be rejected (fail = rejected? Actually \"negative 必须全部拒绝\"). Hmm need semantics. For anchors: positive cases must all pass; negative cases must all be \"rejected\" (refused). Let me define anchor as a field `anchor: 'positive'|'negative'|null`. For positive anchor, all trials must pass. For negative anchor, all trials must be \"rejected\" (a distinct outcome). Violation → gate=fail.\n- gate: if completeCaseCount < minCases (20) → gate = 'insufficient-sample'. Else if any anchor/safety violation → 'fail'. Else if flake rate exceeds threshold or nonSecurityFlakeRateMax exceeded → 'fail'? \"非安全flake≤1%\" - so non-security flake rate ≤1% else fail. Else 'pass'.\n\nLet me carefully define trial outcome values. I'll use:\n- 'pass'\n- 'fail'\n- 'rejected' — for negative anchor \"拒绝\" (refusal). Hmm but also a fail.\n- 'skip'\n- 'timeout'\n- 'crash'\n- 'missing'\n\nLet me define trial outcome as a status string; completion status: pass/fail/rejected are \"completed\"; skip/timeout/crash/missing are \"incomplete\".\n\nActually wait, for negative case, \"拒绝\" (rejected/refused) is the desired outcome. So rejected is a valid completed outcome for negative anchors. For pass^k on negative anchor, we want all trials to be 'rejected'? Hmm. The design says negative anchors must all be rejected. But pass^k is defined as \"全部 k 次通过的完整 case 比例\". Let me interpret: for a case, \"pass\" means the expected outcome for that case. For positive anchor expected = 'pass'; for negative anchor expected = 'rejected'. So a trial \"succeeds\" if it matches the case's expected outcome. Hmm, but that complicates. Let me keep it simpler and more literal to the spec:\n\nActually re-reading: \"正/负锚点：positive 必须全通过、negative 必须全部拒绝；违反即 gate=fail。\" So positive anchor: every trial passes. negative anchor: every trial rejected. I'll treat trial values as 'pass'/'fail'/'rejected' plus incomplete reasons. For pass^k counting, a \"trial success\" = trial value in {'pass','rejected'}? No — that conflates. Let me think about what a \"pass\" means generically.\n\nHmm, simplest consistent model: each case declares `expect: 'allow' | 'deny'`. trial outcome encodes what happened: 'allow' | 'deny' | 'error' | ... Actually the anchors are about \"良性放行/恶意拒绝\" (benign allow / malicious deny). So:\n- positive anchor = benign case expected allow (pass).\n- negative anchor = malicious case expected deny (rejected).\n\nLet me model trial outcome as one of: 'pass', 'fail', 'rejected', 'skip', 'timeout', 'crash', 'missing'. Where:\n- 'pass' = expected-success observed\n- 'rejected' = expected-refusal/block observed (used for negative anchor expected behavior)\n- 'fail' = wrong outcome observed\n\nBut mixing is messy for pass^k. Let me define per-case `expectedOutcome: 'allow' | 'deny'`. Trial `outcome: 'allow' | 'deny' | 'skip' | 'timeout' | 'crash' | 'missing'`. A trial \"succeeds\" when outcome === expectedOutcome. Then:\n- pass^k = fraction of complete cases where all k trials succeed.\n- pass@k = fraction of complete cases where at least one trial succeeds.\n- positive anchor: expectedOutcome='allow'; must all succeed → all trials 'allow'.\n- negative anchor: expectedOutcome='deny'; must all succeed → all trials 'deny' (rejected).\n- safety must-test: cases marked security:true with expectedOutcome='deny' typically; 0 skip / 0 flake.\n\nThat's clean and general. \"拒绝\" = deny. \"全通过\" = all allow.\n\nflake: first trial failed (outcome !== expected) but a later fixed re-run succeeded (outcome === expected). \"后续固定重跑成功\" = subsequent fixed re-run success. So flake := exists a complete case where trial[0] is a wrong outcome (not expected, but completed, i.e., 'allow' vs 'deny' mismatch — not incomplete) AND some trial after index 0 matches expected.\n\n\"保留首次失败\" = retain the first failure (don't overwrite). So the report lists per-case firstTrialOutcome and doesn't rewrite it. And \"重试成功不洗掉原门失败\" = retry success doesn't wash the gate's original failure — i.e., flake case still counts as failing pass^k.\n\nLet me define the aggregation output:\n```\n{\n  gate: 'pass' | 'fail' | 'insufficient-sample',\n  reason: string,\n  k, minCases,\n  rawCaseCount (total cases seen),\n  completeCaseCount (denominator),\n  incompleteCaseCount,\n  incompleteCases: [{id, reason, trials, security, anchor}],\n  passPowK: number|null,\n  passAtK: number|null,\n  perTrialSuccessRate: number|null,\n  flakeRate: number|null,\n  flakeCases: [{id, firstTrial, retryTrialIndex}],\n  nonSecurityFlakeRate,\n  security: {caseCount, skipCount, flakeCount},\n  anchors: {positive:{count, violations:[]}, negative:{count, violations:[]}},\n  excluded:\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nK is fixed (k=3). If a case has fewer than k trials → incomplete reason 'missing'. If more than k trials? The design: k trials per case; extra trials might be \"fixed re-runs\" used for flake detection. Hmm. Actually re-read: \"flake = 首次 trial 失败、后续固定重跑成功的 case 比例\". So the case may have first trial plus re-runs. So the trial array could be more than k. Let me define: case has `trials` array; for pass^k we require exactly k trials present (the first k). Re-runs beyond k are separate `reruns` used only for flake. Hmm, but \"后续固定重跑\" could be within trials.\n\nLet me simplify and make it explicit in fixture: each case has `trials: [...]` of length k for the primary run, and an optional `reruns: [...]`. Actually cleaner: case.trials holds ALL attempts in order; the first k complete-ish attempts count for pass^k; subsequent are re-runs. Hmm, but if trial[0] incomplete, then...\n\nLet me define it precisely and document:\n- A case has `trials`: ordered list of attempt outcomes. The first `k` entries are the design trials used for pass^k / pass@k. Any additional entries are fixed re-runs used only for flake detection.\n- If the first k trials contain an incomplete outcome, OR fewer than k entries exist, the case is incomplete → excluded from denominator, retained with reason = the first incomplete reason (or 'missing' if fewer than k).\n- complete case: first k trials all completed (allow/deny) and length >= k.\n- pass^k success: all first k trials === expected.\n- pass@k: at least one of first k === expected.\n- flake: trial[0] is a completed wrong outcome AND any trial at index >= 1 (including re-runs) === expected. First failure retained.\n\nWait, but what if trial[0] is correct but a later trial is wrong? Not flake. Flake requires first fail then later success.\n\nHmm, but re-run success could also be within a rerun set where the \"first trial failed\" - the first of the whole array.\n\nActually let me reconsider: maybe simpler that a case has exactly k trials, and flake uses a separate `rerun` field. But the spec says \"首次 trial 失败、后续固定重跑成功\". So the pattern: case's first trial fails; a fixed re-run (deterministic rerun) succeeds. Let me model `trials` includes reruns appended. I'll go with: first k = primary trials; extras = fixed reruns.\n\nHmm, but then flake detection with extras. Let me just make flake: index 0 completed-wrong AND ∃ i>=1 with trials[i]===expected. This covers reruns appended.\n\nActually wait — is it possible for flake to happen with i in [1,k)? If first k all must be same-run? The design says \"同候选固定重跑后由初次失败变成功的case比例\" — initial failure becomes success after fixed rerun on same candidate. So the rerun is a separate run, not part of the k trials. So flake should use reruns, not the k trials.\n\nHmm. But \"首次 trial 失败\" says \"first trial failed\". Let me define: the case's primary trials are the k trials. `reruns` is an optional array of fixed re-run outcomes. flake = case where primary trial[0] is a completed wrong outcome AND some rerun === expected. That's the cleanest mapping of \"首次trial失败、后续固定重跑成功\". And \"保留首次失败\" means we keep primary trial[0] as the recorded failure.\n\nWait, but \"首次 trial 失败\" - the first of the k trials. Actually for pass^k, ALL k must pass, so if the first fails, pass^k already fails regardless. Retry success doesn't change that. And flake is a separate metric for reporting instability.\n\nLet me go with: case.trials = array length k (primary), case.reruns = optional array. Actually I realize to be safe and general, let me support both: if trials length > k, extras beyond k are treated as reruns appended. So `allTrials = trials`; primary = trials.slice(0,k); reruns = trials.slice(k) concat case.reruns. This handles fixture data naturally.\n\nActually, let me keep it simple and well-documented: `trials` is the ordered primary sequence of exactly the design runs (length should be k); extra entries are re-runs. Plus optional explicit `reruns`. Hmm, over-engineering. Let me just do: case.trials (primary attempts, length k expected) and case.reruns (fixed reruns). I'll document clearly.\n\nHmm, re-reading one more: \"flake = 首次 trial 失败、后续固定重跑成功的 case 比例\". Given \"首次 trial\" = first trial. I'll define flake precisely as: first primary trial is a completed wrong outcome, and at least one subsequent attempt (in remaining primary trials or reruns) is the expected outcome. This is a superset that handles both interpretations and is defensible. Actually no — \"后续固定重跑\" specifically means fixed re-run; if a later primary trial succeeds, that would be within the same k-run set. Since pass^k already requires all k, a case with trial[0]=wrong and trial[1]=expected fails pass^k anyway. Counting it as flake is reasonable. But to match \"首次trial失败、后续固定重跑成功\" precisely, I'll count: first attempt wrong AND any later attempt expected. Good.\n\nLet me just implement with a single `trials` array and treat index 0 specially, and define `reruns` as optional additional attempts concatenated after the first k. I'll make the fixture include a rerun case to exercise it.\n\nGate rules:\n1. If completeCaseCount < minCases → gate='insufficient-sample' (never PASS on small sample).\n2. Else if security violations (security case with any incomplete/skip OR security flake > budget 0) → 'fail'.\n3. Else if anchor violations (positive not all expected; negative not all deny/expected... well expected=deny) → 'fail'.\n4. Else if nonSecurityFlakeRate > nonSecurityFlakeRateMax (0.01) → 'fail'.\n5. Else 'pass'.\n\nHmm also flake gate: security flake budget 0, non-security ≤ 0.01. Also security skip budget 0.\n\nAlso \"安全必测项 0 skip/0 flake\" — so if any security case has a skip-type incomplete, fail. Even if security case is incomplete, it's excluded from denominator but the skip/0 rule triggers fail. Actually incomplete case excluded from denominators, but security skip counts as a violation → fail.\n\nLet me finalize the gate. Order: insufficient-sample takes precedence? The acceptance says \"完整 case 数低于设计门（k=3、>=20）时 gate=insufficient-sample，绝不以小样本冒充 PASS\". And anchor/safety violations → fail. Which takes precedence if both? I'll make safety/anchor fail take precedence over insufficient-sample? Hmm. Design: \"违反即 gate=fail\". I'll compute: if completeCaseCount < minCases → base = 'insufficient-sample'. But if there are hard violations (safety/anchor), report 'fail' because a violation is definitive regardless of sample. Hmm, but a violation on a tiny sample... Let me make safety/anchor violations → 'fail' prioritized, else insufficient-sample, else