gative', security?: boolean, expectedOutcome: 'allow'|'deny', trials: [outcome,...], reruns?: [outcome,...] }\n\nWait, let me reconsider expectedOutcome vs anchors. Let me simplify: each case has `expected: 'pass'|'reject'`. Hmm, \"positive 必须全通过、negative 必须全部拒绝\". So positive anchor → expected pass; negative anchor → expected reject. Let me name trial outcomes: 'pass' | 'fail' | 'rejected'? Ugh.\n\nCleaner naming aligned with domain: trial outcome ∈ {'passed','failed','rejected','skipped','timeout','crash','missing'}. Hmm, but 'rejected' for negative anchor is a *success*. Confusing.\n\nLet me use neutral: case.expected ∈ {'allow','deny'}; trial.outcome ∈ {'allow','deny','skip','timeout','crash','missing'}. Success when outcome === expected. Incomplete when outcome ∈ {skip,timeout,crash,missing}. This is unambiguous. The \"positive anchor must all pass\" = expected 'allow' and all trials 'allow'. \"negative must all reject\" = expected 'deny' and all trials 'deny'. \n\nBut wait, \"positive\" in the safety eval sense = benign should be allowed; \"negative\" = malicious should be denied. Yes! That matches \"正/负锚点\" and \"良性放行/恶意拒绝\". \n\nNow incomplete outcomes: 'skip','timeout','crash','missing'. Also maybe 'error'. I'll support these.\n\nLet me define constants and validation.\n\nAggregate:\n```\nfunction aggregateReliability({ params, cases, sourceLabel, generatedAt }) {\n  normalize params (k>=1 int, minCases>=1, budgets)\n  for each case:\n    primary = case.trials.slice(0,k)\n    if primary.length < k -> incomplete reason 'missing'\n    else first incomplete outcome in primary -> incomplete reason that outcome (skip/timeout/crash/missing)\n    else complete\n  completeCases = [...]\n  incomplete = [...]\n  passPowK = complete.filter(all primary === expected).length / complete.length  (null if 0)\n  passAtK = complete.filter(some primary === expected).length / complete.length\n  perTrialSuccessRate = across all complete cases' primary trials: successes / total\n  flakes: complete cases where primary[0] !== expected (and is not incomplete—it's completed wrong) AND any later attempt (primary[1..] + reruns) === expected\n  flakeRate = flakes.length / complete.length\n  nonSecurityComplete = complete.filter(!security)\n  nonSecurityFlakeRate = flakes(non-security).length / nonSecurityComplete.length\n  security stats: securityComplete count, security incomplete/skip count, security flakes count\n  anchors: positive/negative with violations (complete cases not all expected) + also incomplete anchor cases are violations? \"positive必须全通过\" — if a positive anchor case is incomplete, it didn't pass → violation. So anchor violation includes incomplete anchor cases.\n  gate + reasons\n}\n```\n\nWait, for anchors: should incomplete anchor case count as violation? \"必须全通过\" — an incomplete case didn't pass, so yes violation. Let me treat incomplete anchor as violation (can't confirm pass → fail). Reasonable and safe.\n\nSecurity incomplete (skip) → violation (0 skip).\n\nGate:\n```\nreasons = []\nif hardViolations (anchorViolation || securityViolation) -> gate='fail', reasons\nelse if completeCaseCount < minCases -> gate='insufficient-sample'\nelse if nonSecurityFlakeRate > max -> gate='fail'\nelse gate='pass'\n```\nHmm precedence: if both insufficient and hard violation? Let me put hard violations first since they are definitive. Actually, acceptance #5: \"完整 case 数低于设计门时 gate=insufficient-sample\". If there's also an anchor violation... ambiguous. I'll prioritize hard violation = 'fail' (a violation is worse). Hmm, but then a tiny sample with a violation shows 'fail' not 'insufficient-sample'. I think that's fine and safe. But to respect #5 strictly, maybe insufficient-sample should be checked first. Let me check: #5 says when complete < minCases → insufficient-sample, \"绝不以小样本冒充 PASS\". It doesn't forbid 'fail'. If there's a violation on a small sample, 'fail' is still not PASS. I'll do: hard violation → 'fail'; else insufficient → 'insufficient-sample'; else flake → fail/pass. This ensures violations always surface.\n\nHmm, actually maybe better: gate='insufficient-sample' when under-sampled AND no hard violation; if under-sampled AND hard violation → 'fail'. Yes that's what I said. Good.\n\nNow the report structure for the benefit report integration. The benefit report currently has {generatedAt, durationMs, agentDev, quality, selectorCoverage, externalTools}. I'll add `reliability` key with the aggregate + fixture provenance, and add a markdown section.\n\nLet me also add fixture provenance: source: 'deterministic-local-fixture', liveProvider:false, realHuman:false, driven_by:'agent'.\n\nNow let me be careful: the benefit eval script must not break when fixture missing? It's committed, so fine. But guard with a friendly error.\n\nNow the `d.mts` file: pattern `/* Auto-generated test typing surface for reliability-aggregation.mjs */` + `export const X: any;` for each export.\n\nNow unit test: `tests/unit/agent-dev-reliability-aggregation.test.ts`. It imports from '../../scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs' and the fixture JSON. It should be node environment. Use `// @vitest-environment node`. Tests:\n1. fixture declares pre-registered k=3, minCases>=20, thresholds; fixture is deterministic oracle labeled synthetic/local.\n2. oracle aggregate: gate pass, completeCaseCount>=20, incomplete retained with reasons, pass^k and pass@k distinct, flake 0, anchors satisfied, security 0 skip/0 flake.\n3. pass^k vs pass@k: constructed case where one trial passes → passAtK counts, passPowK excludes.\n4. incomplete excluded from denominator but retained.\n5. flake: first fail + rerun success counted, and pass^k still fails for that case; flake doesn't wash original failure.\n6. anchor violation → gate fail.\n7. security skip → gate fail; security flake → gate fail.\n8. insufficient sample → gate insufficient-sample.\n9. report contains raw denominator, pass^k, pass@k, flake, missing, gate, reasons.\n10. Also test the benefit-eval integration? Could run the script? That needs node child process but no deps — vitest can spawn node. Might be heavy. Instead, I'll test that the benefit report renderer includes reliability. Hmm, the benefit script writes files. Could import functions from it? It's a top-level script (side-effectful). Better: refactor so aggregation is in lib, and benefit script just calls it. Test the lib + fixture. I can also run the script via child_process in the test to assert the report.json contains reliability.gate. That's a real integration test but spawns node (fast, no deps). Let me include it but guard: it writes artifacts/agent-dev-benefit-eval. Acceptable. Actually writing artifacts during tests may be undesirable. Hmm. The test could run with a temp cwd? The script uses process.cwd() as ROOT. If I spawn with cwd=tmp, it'll fail reading shared files. Alternatively I can `import` the script — but it executes on import. Let me just test the lib and fixture thoroughly, and separately verify the script by running it as part of my manual checks (not in vitest). Keep the unit test dependency-free and deterministic.\n\nHmm, but I want the test to cover \"报告同时给出 ...\". I'll have the lib produce the report object and assert those fields. And the benefit script passes the fixture through. Good enough. Actually, let me add one test that imports the benefit-eval module? Since it's side-effectful, skip. I'll rely on running the script manually.\n\nLet me now think about the `.d.mts` — is it required by tsconfig.test? The test imports the .mjs. TS with allowJs? tsconfig.web has no allowJs; importing .mjs from .ts test... The other tests import '../../scripts/contract-check.mjs' and there are .d.mts files for those. So TS resolves .mjs imports via the sibling .d.mts declaration. So I MUST provide reliability-aggregation.d.mts for typecheck. Good — that's why it's in scope.\n\nLet me check contract-check.d.mts to mirror style.\n\nNow the harness task spec